    <article id="lesson-article"><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-al-ict-lesson-10-web-development-1"><h1 id="al-ict-lesson-10-web-development-1">AL ICT – Lesson 10 Web Development</h1><h2 id="full-summary-note-for-a-static-student-learning-web-page-2">Full Summary Note for a Static Student Learning Web Page</h2><h3 id="source-restriction-3">Source restriction</h3><p>This content is based <strong>only on the two supplied lesson source files</strong>:</p><ol><li><strong>LESSON 10 (PART 01) - HTML &amp; CSS (2026 NEW).pdf</strong></li><li><strong>LESSON 10 (PART 02) - PHP (2026 NEW).pdf</strong></li></ol><p>Do not add unrelated technologies, concepts, or explanations from outside sources when converting this note into the student web page.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-part-01-html-css-4"><h1 id="part-01-html-css-4">PART 01 – HTML &amp; CSS</h1><h2 id="1-html-hypertext-markup-language-5">1. HTML – HyperText Markup Language</h2><h3 id="what-is-html-6">What is HTML?</h3><p>HTML is a markup language used to write instructions for web browsers about how website content should be displayed.</p><p>HTML describes the:</p><ul><li>structure of a webpage</li><li>text content</li><li>media</li><li>links</li><li>tables</li><li>forms</li><li>other webpage elements</li></ul><p>HTML is mainly responsible for the <strong>structure and content</strong> of a webpage.</p><h3 id="important-people-components-in-web-development-7">Important people/components in web development</h3><p><strong>User</strong></p><ul><li>A person who interacts with a website or web application using a computer, smartphone, or another device.</li></ul><p><strong>Developer</strong></p><ul><li>A person who writes programs and creates the structure of websites.</li><li>The developer provides instructions that tell browsers how to render content.</li></ul><p><strong>Web Server</strong></p><ul><li>A computer that stores website content and serves it to users' browsers through the Internet.</li></ul><p><strong>Web Browser</strong></p><ul><li>Software used to request, view, navigate, and interact with web content.</li><li>Examples in the source: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge, Safari and Opera.</li></ul><p><strong>Web Request</strong></p><ul><li>A message sent by a browser to a web server requesting a particular webpage or resource.</li></ul><p><strong>Web Response</strong></p><ul><li>The server's reply to a web request.</li><li>It contains the requested website content and instructions, including HTML, for the browser.</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-2-editing-software-for-html-8"><h1 id="2-editing-software-for-html-8">2. Editing Software for HTML</h1><h2 id="text-editors-9">Text Editors</h2><p>A text editor is simple software used to create and edit plain-text files.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Notepad</li><li>TextEdit</li><li>Gedit</li><li>Nano</li></ul><h2 id="code-editors-10">Code Editors</h2><p>A code editor is a specialized text editor for writing and editing programming code.</p><p>Useful features include:</p><ul><li>syntax highlighting</li><li>auto-completion</li><li>code-editing assistance</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Visual Studio Code</li><li>Sublime Text</li><li>Atom</li><li>Notepad++</li><li>Brackets</li><li>Vim</li></ul><h2 id="word-processors-11">Word Processors</h2><p>Word processors are mainly designed for creating, formatting and styling documents containing text, images, tables and charts.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>MS Word</li><li>Google Docs</li><li>LibreOffice</li><li>WPS</li></ul><p>A word processor does not provide the features required for efficient coding in the same way a code editor does.</p><h3 id="saving-an-html-file-12">Saving an HTML file</h3><p>After writing HTML code:</p><ul><li>save the file using the <code>.html</code> extension</li><li>example: <code>example.html</code></li></ul><h3 id="viewing-an-html-file-13">Viewing an HTML file</h3><ol><li>Save the HTML file.</li><li>Open the file using a web browser.</li><li>The browser reads and renders the HTML code.</li><li>The resulting webpage is displayed.</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-3-web-browser-and-search-engine-14"><h1 id="3-web-browser-and-search-engine-14">3. Web Browser and Search Engine</h1><h2 id="web-browser-15">Web Browser</h2><p>A web browser:</p><ul><li>accesses websites</li><li>retrieves webpages from web servers</li><li>displays webpage content</li><li>allows users to navigate and interact with websites</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Google Chrome</li><li>Mozilla Firefox</li><li>Microsoft Edge</li><li>Safari</li><li>Opera</li></ul><h2 id="search-engine-16">Search Engine</h2><p>A search engine helps users find websites and information.</p><p>It:</p><ol><li>scans web content</li><li>stores information</li><li>receives keywords from users</li><li>provides relevant results</li></ol><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Google</li><li>Bing</li><li>Yahoo</li><li>DuckDuckGo</li><li>Ecosia</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-4-network-internet-and-www-17"><h1 id="4-network-internet-and-www-17">4. Network, Internet and WWW</h1><h2 id="network-18">Network</h2><p>A network is a group of connected devices that share data and resources.</p><p>Devices can be connected using:</p><ul><li>cables</li><li>Wi-Fi</li></ul><p>Networking devices mentioned in the source:</p><ul><li>Hub</li><li>Switch</li><li>Router</li></ul><h2 id="internet-19">Internet</h2><p>The Internet is a worldwide network of connected computers and devices that allows people to share information and communicate.</p><p>The Internet:</p><ul><li>is not owned by one person or organization</li><li>consists of many interconnected networks</li></ul><h2 id="world-wide-web-www-20">World Wide Web (WWW)</h2><p>The WWW is a system of linked webpages and multimedia content accessed through the Internet.</p><p>It:</p><ul><li>allows information to be shared through linked webpages</li><li>uses hyperlinks/Hypertext</li><li>is accessed using web browsers</li></ul><h3 id="history-of-www-21">History of WWW</h3><ul><li>WWW was created as a project by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989 at CERN.</li><li>The project aimed to help researchers collaborate and share information.</li><li>W3C was later established to advance web development and create web standards and guidelines.</li></ul><h2 id="typical-webpage-contents-22">Typical webpage contents</h2><p>A webpage may contain:</p><ul><li>text</li><li>headings</li><li>paragraphs</li><li>images</li><li>graphics</li><li>animations</li><li>video</li><li>audio</li><li>hyperlinks</li><li>forms</li><li>buttons</li><li>navigation menus</li><li>footer</li><li>tables</li><li>lists</li><li>icons</li><li>search bars</li><li>slideshows</li><li>chat widgets</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-5-url-uniform-resource-locator-23"><h1 id="5-url-uniform-resource-locator-23">5. URL – Uniform Resource Locator</h1><p>A URL is the address of a webpage on the Internet.</p><p>It helps the browser locate and open a website.</p><h3 id="main-parts-described-in-the-source-24">Main parts described in the source</h3><p><strong>Protocol</strong></p><ul><li>A set of rules and standards used for communication over a network.</li><li>Example: <code>https:</code></li></ul><p><strong>Domain Name</strong></p><ul><li>A human-readable name that identifies a website.</li><li>It is used instead of a numerical IP address.</li></ul><p><strong>Path</strong></p><ul><li>Identifies a particular page or location within a website.</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-6-www-vs-internet-25"><h1 id="6-www-vs-internet-25">6. WWW vs Internet</h1><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>WWW</th><th>Internet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Originated in 1989 according to the source</td><td>Originated in 1960 according to the source</td></tr><tr><td>Interconnected webpages and documents accessed through the Internet</td><td>Worldwide infrastructure/network connecting computers and devices</td></tr><tr><td>Uses protocols such as HTTP</td><td>Uses protocols such as TCP/IP</td></tr><tr><td>Based mainly on software/web services</td><td>Based on the underlying network infrastructure</td></tr><tr><td>A service contained inside the Internet infrastructure</td><td>The infrastructure that supports network communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-7-types-of-websites-26"><h1 id="7-types-of-websites-26">7. Types of Websites</h1><h2 id="1-news-and-information-websites-27">1. News and Information Websites</h2><p>Provide current events, trends and general knowledge.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>BBC</li><li>CNN</li><li>New York Times</li></ul><h2 id="2-personal-websites-28">2. Personal Websites</h2><p>Created by individuals to share:</p><ul><li>personal information</li><li>blogs</li><li>portfolios</li><li>opinions</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>WordPress blogs</li><li>Medium</li></ul><h2 id="3-educational-websites-29">3. Educational Websites</h2><p>Provide:</p><ul><li>learning materials</li><li>courses</li><li>tutorials</li><li>academic resources</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Khan Academy</li><li>Coursera</li><li>university websites</li></ul><h2 id="4-business-websites-30">4. Business Websites</h2><p>Promote products/services and provide company information.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Microsoft</li><li>Apple</li></ul><h2 id="5-research-websites-31">5. Research Websites</h2><p>Share:</p><ul><li>academic studies</li><li>research papers</li><li>scientific findings</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>ResearchGate</li><li>PubMed</li></ul><h2 id="6-entertainment-websites-32">6. Entertainment Websites</h2><p>Provide:</p><ul><li>movies</li><li>music</li><li>games</li><li>entertainment content</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>YouTube</li><li>Netflix</li><li>Spotify</li></ul><h2 id="7-social-media-websites-33">7. Social Media Websites</h2><p>Allow users to:</p><ul><li>interact</li><li>share content</li><li>connect with others</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Facebook</li><li>Instagram</li><li>LinkedIn</li></ul><h2 id="8-e-commerce-websites-34">8. E-commerce Websites</h2><p>Allow users to buy and sell products or services online.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Amazon</li><li>eBay</li><li>Shopify</li></ul><h2 id="9-government-websites-35">9. Government Websites</h2><p>Provide:</p><ul><li>public services</li><li>official information</li><li>announcements</li></ul><h2 id="10-web-portals-36">10. Web Portals</h2><p>Provide several services in one place.</p><p>Examples of services:</p><ul><li>email</li><li>news</li><li>search</li><li>user accounts</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Yahoo</li><li>MSN</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-8-user-requirements-and-website-objectives-37"><h1 id="8-user-requirements-and-website-objectives-37">8. User Requirements and Website Objectives</h1><p>Before creating a website, the developer should understand why the website is needed and what users expect.</p><h2 id="questions-to-ask-38">Questions to ask</h2><h3 id="why-is-a-new-website-needed-39">Why is a new website needed?</h3><p>Identify the main reason for creating the website.</p><h3 id="what-outcomes-should-be-achieved-40">What outcomes should be achieved?</h3><p>Possible objectives mentioned include:</p><ul><li>increasing brand awareness</li><li>increasing sales</li><li>generating leads</li><li>improving customer service</li><li>achieving another specific organizational goal</li></ul><h3 id="how-will-the-user-experience-be-improved-41">How will the user experience be improved?</h3><p>Possible areas:</p><ul><li>website speed</li><li>content clarity</li><li>navigation</li><li>mobile-friendly design</li></ul><h3 id="what-should-be-improved-through-the-website-42">What should be improved through the website?</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>customer engagement</li><li>sales conversion</li><li>information delivery</li></ul><h3 id="is-there-an-existing-website-43">Is there an existing website?</h3><p>Identify existing problems and areas for improvement.</p><h3 id="should-new-opportunities-be-communicated-44">Should new opportunities be communicated?</h3><p>Determine whether the website should provide updates and opportunities.</p><h3 id="is-the-organization-planning-to-expand-45">Is the organization planning to expand?</h3><p>The website should be designed with future growth and scalability in mind.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-9-setting-website-goals-46"><h1 id="9-setting-website-goals-46">9. Setting Website Goals</h1><p>Examples in the source:</p><h2 id="increasing-enquiries-47">Increasing enquiries</h2><p>A service website may set a measurable goal to increase enquiries.</p><p>Possible methods:</p><ul><li>improve contact forms</li><li>improve call-to-action buttons</li><li>make contact information easier to find</li></ul><h2 id="increasing-online-sales-48">Increasing online sales</h2><p>An e-commerce website may set a measurable sales goal.</p><p>Possible methods:</p><ul><li>promotions</li><li>better product descriptions</li><li>improved checkout process</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-10-identifying-key-audiences-49"><h1 id="10-identifying-key-audiences-49">10. Identifying Key Audiences</h1><p>A website should identify its main users.</p><h2 id="potential-customers-50">Potential Customers</h2><p>People or businesses that may buy products or services.</p><p>The website should consider their:</p><ul><li>needs</li><li>preferences</li><li>expected information</li></ul><h2 id="current-customers-51">Current Customers</h2><p>Existing customers may use the website for:</p><ul><li>support</li><li>updates</li><li>repeat purchases</li></ul><h2 id="employees-52">Employees</h2><p>Employees may use a website for:</p><ul><li>internal resources</li><li>internal communication</li><li>work-related tasks</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-11-prioritizing-audience-needs-53"><h1 id="11-prioritizing-audience-needs-53">11. Prioritizing Audience Needs</h1><p>Audience needs influence:</p><ul><li>layout</li><li>navigation</li><li>content strategy</li></ul><p>For example, if potential customers are the main audience, the website should emphasize:</p><ul><li>clear product information</li><li>easy navigation</li><li>prominent contact options</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-12-gathering-user-requirements-54"><h1 id="12-gathering-user-requirements-54">12. Gathering User Requirements</h1><p>User requirements describe what the website needs to do.</p><p>Requirements can change over time, so websites need regular updates.</p><p>Important requirement areas:</p><h2 id="content-55">Content</h2><p>Decide whether the website needs:</p><ul><li>text</li><li>images</li><li>videos</li><li>blogs</li><li>product descriptions</li></ul><h2 id="layout-and-navigation-56">Layout and Navigation</h2><p>Plan:</p><ul><li>overall page layout</li><li>navigation structure</li><li>how users move through the website</li></ul><h2 id="usability-57">Usability</h2><p>Consider:</p><ul><li>accessibility</li><li>responsive design</li><li>alternative text for images</li><li>keyboard navigation</li></ul><h2 id="security-58">Security</h2><p>Identify website security requirements.</p><h2 id="loading-times-59">Loading Times</h2><p>Determine acceptable loading times. Fast loading supports user satisfaction and can affect search-engine rankings.</p><h2 id="legal-considerations-60">Legal Considerations</h2><p>Consider:</p><ul><li>privacy policies</li><li>terms of service</li><li>copyright</li><li>accessibility standards</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-13-basic-html-structure-61"><h1 id="13-basic-html-structure-61">13. Basic HTML Structure</h1><p>HTML tags are the building blocks of an HTML document.</p><p>A tag is written using angle brackets.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;p&gt;Hello World&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre><p>An HTML element consists of the tags and the content they contain.</p><h2 id="basic-html-document-62">Basic HTML document</h2><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;My First HTML Page&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    Content
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><h3 id="doctype-html-63"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code></h3><p>Declares that the document uses HTML5.</p><h3 id="html-64"><code>&lt;html&gt;</code></h3><p>Root element of the HTML document.</p><h3 id="head-65"><code>&lt;head&gt;</code></h3><p>Contains metadata about the document.</p><h3 id="title-66"><code>&lt;title&gt;</code></h3><p>Defines the title shown on the browser tab.</p><h3 id="body-67"><code>&lt;body&gt;</code></h3><p>Contains the main visible webpage content.</p><h2 id="basic-html-rules-from-the-source-68">Basic HTML rules from the source</h2><ul><li>HTML is not case-sensitive.</li><li>HTML files should use <code>.html</code> or <code>.htm</code>.</li><li>Browsers may ignore HTML errors.</li><li>Extra spaces and line breaks are generally ignored.</li><li>HTML follows a hierarchical structure.</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-14-types-of-html-tags-69"><h1 id="14-types-of-html-tags-69">14. Types of HTML Tags</h1><h2 id="container-tags-70">Container Tags</h2><p>Container tags normally have an opening and closing tag and contain content.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Hello World&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="empty-tags-self-closing-tags-71">Empty Tags / Self-Closing Tags</h2><p>These do not require a separate closing tag.</p><p>Examples:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;img&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;input&gt;
&lt;link&gt;
&lt;meta&gt;</code></pre><p>They are commonly used for:</p><ul><li>images</li><li>line breaks/rules</li><li>input controls</li><li>linked resources</li><li>metadata</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-15-character-entities-72"><h1 id="15-character-entities-72">15. Character Entities</h1><p>Character entities represent special characters that cannot always be typed directly in HTML.</p><p>They are written using:</p><ul><li><code>&amp;name;</code></li><li><code>&amp;#code;</code></li></ul><p>Examples:</p><ul><li><code>&amp;nbsp;</code> – non-breaking space</li><li><code>&amp;copy;</code> – copyright symbol</li><li><code>&amp;#169;</code> – copyright using a numeric code</li><li><code>&amp;apos;</code> – apostrophe</li><li><code>&amp;quot;</code> – double quotation mark</li><li><code>&amp;amp;</code> – ampersand</li><li><code>&amp;reg;</code> – registered trademark</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-16-text-formatting-tags-73"><h1 id="16-text-formatting-tags-73">16. Text Formatting Tags</h1><p>Important tags in the source:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;b&gt;</code> – bold text</li><li><code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> – important/strongly emphasized text</li><li><code>&lt;u&gt;</code> – underline</li><li><code>&lt;ins&gt;</code> – inserted text</li><li><code>&lt;i&gt;</code> – italic text</li><li><code>&lt;em&gt;</code> – emphasized text</li><li><code>&lt;s&gt;</code> – strikethrough</li><li><code>&lt;strike&gt;</code> – older/deprecated strikethrough tag</li><li><code>&lt;del&gt;</code> – deleted content</li><li><code>&lt;big&gt;</code> – larger text</li><li><code>&lt;small&gt;</code> – smaller text</li><li><code>&lt;sup&gt;</code> – superscript</li><li><code>&lt;sub&gt;</code> – subscript</li><li><code>&lt;mark&gt;</code> – highlighted text</li><li><code>&lt;br&gt;</code> – line break</li></ul><p>Examples:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;b&gt;Bold&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Important&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;u&gt;Underline&lt;/u&gt;
&lt;i&gt;Italic&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;s&gt;Deleted-looking text&lt;/s&gt;
&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;
&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;
&lt;mark&gt;Highlight&lt;/mark&gt;
&lt;br&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-17-html-comments-74"><h1 id="17-html-comments-74">17. HTML Comments</h1><p>HTML comments are notes written in source code that are not displayed in the browser.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Comment --&gt;</code></pre><p>Uses:</p><ul><li>explain code</li><li>temporarily disable code</li><li>improve code readability</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-18-font-tag-75"><h1 id="18-font-tag-75">18. <code>&lt;font&gt;</code> Tag</h1><p>The source introduces the <code>&lt;font&gt;</code> tag for changing text appearance.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;font color=&quot;blue&quot; face=&quot;Arial&quot; size=&quot;4&quot;&gt;
    Styled text
&lt;/font&gt;</code></pre><p>Important attributes:</p><ul><li><code>color</code></li><li><code>face</code></li><li><code>size</code></li></ul><h2 id="color-names-76">Color names</h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>red</li><li>blue</li><li>green</li></ul><h2 id="hexadecimal-colors-77">Hexadecimal colors</h2><p>Format:</p><pre><code class="language-text">#RRGGBB</code></pre><p>Each pair ranges from <code>00</code> to <code>FF</code>.</p><p>Examples:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;font color=&quot;#FF0000&quot;&gt;Red Text&lt;/font&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="rgb-colors-78">RGB colors</h2><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;font color=&quot;rgb(255,0,0)&quot;&gt;Red Text&lt;/font&gt;</code></pre><p>The source also describes:</p><ul><li><code>bgcolor</code> in the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tag for setting webpage background color</li><li><code>face</code> for font family</li><li><code>size</code> values from 1 to 7</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-19-predefined-and-customizable-tags-79"><h1 id="19-predefined-and-customizable-tags-79">19. Predefined and Customizable Tags</h1><h2 id="predefined-tags-80">Predefined Tags</h2><p>Basic tags that can work without additional attributes.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code></li><li><code>&lt;b&gt;</code></li><li><code>&lt;i&gt;</code></li></ul><h2 id="customizable-tags-81">Customizable Tags</h2><p>Tags that support attributes.</p><p>An attribute provides additional information or controls an element.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">class=&quot;example&quot;
id=&quot;section&quot;</code></pre><p>An attribute has:</p><ol><li>attribute name</li><li>attribute value</li></ol><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">color=&quot;blue&quot;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-20-paragraph-tag-82"><h1 id="20-paragraph-tag-82">20. Paragraph Tag</h1><p>The <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag defines a paragraph.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre><p>The source also describes the <code>align</code> attribute:</p><ul><li>left</li><li>right</li><li>center</li><li>justify</li></ul><p>The source notes that <code>align</code> is deprecated in HTML5.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-21-pre-tag-83"><h1 id="21-pre-tag-83">21. <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> Tag</h1><p>The <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> tag displays preformatted text.</p><p>It preserves:</p><ul><li>spaces</li><li>line breaks</li><li>indentation</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;pre&gt;
Line 1
    Line 2
Line 3
&lt;/pre&gt;</code></pre><p>Content is normally displayed using a monospaced font.</p><h2 id="monospaced-font-84">Monospaced font</h2><p>Each character occupies the same width.</p><p>Useful for:</p><ul><li>code</li><li>ASCII art</li><li>precisely aligned text</li></ul><p>The source contrasts this with:</p><ul><li>Serif fonts</li><li>Sans fonts</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-22-heading-tags-85"><h1 id="22-heading-tags-85">22. Heading Tags</h1><p>HTML provides:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;
&lt;h6&gt;</code></pre><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> = highest/most important heading</li><li><code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> = lowest/least important heading</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;h1&gt;Main Heading&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Subheading&lt;/h2&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="seo-and-headings-86">SEO and headings</h2><p>The source explains that headings help search engines understand webpage structure.</p><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> identifies the main topic.</li><li><code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>–<code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> identify subtopics.</li><li>Meaningful keywords in headings support indexing.</li><li>Proper heading order improves crawl efficiency.</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-23-horizontal-rule-87"><h1 id="23-horizontal-rule-87">23. Horizontal Rule</h1><p>The <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code> tag creates a horizontal line/thematic separation.</p><p>It is a self-closing/empty tag.</p><p>Attributes described in the source:</p><ul><li><code>align</code></li><li><code>color</code></li><li><code>size</code></li><li><code>width</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-24-html-lists-88"><h1 id="24-html-lists-88">24. HTML Lists</h1><p>The source covers three types.</p><h2 id="1-unordered-list-ul-89">1. Unordered List – <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code></h2><p>Used when item order is not important.</p><p>Each item uses <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>.</p><p>Bullet styles:</p><ul><li><code>disc</code></li><li><code>circle</code></li><li><code>square</code></li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Apple&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Banana&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Cherry&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="2-ordered-list-ol-90">2. Ordered List – <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code></h2><p>Used when sequence/order is important.</p><p>Possible numbering styles:</p><ul><li><code>1</code> – numbers</li><li><code>A</code> – uppercase letters</li><li><code>a</code> – lowercase letters</li><li><code>I</code> – uppercase Roman numerals</li><li><code>i</code> – lowercase Roman numerals</li></ul><p>The <code>start</code> attribute specifies the starting value.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;ol type=&quot;1&quot; start=&quot;10&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="3-definition-list-dl-91">3. Definition List – <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code></h2><p>Used for terms and their descriptions.</p><p>Tags:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;dl&gt;</code> – definition list</li><li><code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> – term/topic</li><li><code>&lt;dd&gt;</code> – description/value</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;dl&gt;
    &lt;dt&gt;Python&lt;/dt&gt;
    &lt;dd&gt;Uses Interpreters&lt;/dd&gt;

    &lt;dt&gt;Java&lt;/dt&gt;
    &lt;dd&gt;Uses Compilers&lt;/dd&gt;
&lt;/dl&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-25-div-and-span-92"><h1 id="25-div-and-span-92">25. <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;span&gt;</code></h1><h2 id="div-93"><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></h2><p>A block-level container used to:</p><ul><li>group elements</li><li>organize webpage sections</li><li>structure content</li><li>work with CSS and JavaScript</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;div&gt;
    Content
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="title-attribute-94"><code>title</code> Attribute</h2><p>Provides additional information when the pointer hovers over an element.</p><h2 id="span-95"><code>&lt;span&gt;</code></h2><p>Used to wrap a small part of content so it can be styled without changing the normal display structure.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;p&gt;
    This is
    &lt;span style=&quot;color:red;&quot;&gt;important&lt;/span&gt;
    text.
&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-26-html-tables-96"><h1 id="26-html-tables-96">26. HTML Tables</h1><p>The <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> element creates a table.</p><p>Main table tags:</p><h3 id="table-97"><code>&lt;table&gt;</code></h3><p>Main table container.</p><h3 id="caption-98"><code>&lt;caption&gt;</code></h3><p>Provides a title/description for the table.</p><h3 id="tr-99"><code>&lt;tr&gt;</code></h3><p>Defines a table row.</p><h3 id="td-100"><code>&lt;td&gt;</code></h3><p>Defines a normal table-data cell.</p><h3 id="th-101"><code>&lt;th&gt;</code></h3><p>Defines a header cell. The source notes that <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> is normally bold and centered.</p><h2 id="table-attributes-102">Table attributes</h2><p>For <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>:</p><ul><li><code>border</code></li><li><code>bgcolor</code></li><li><code>align</code></li><li><code>cellpadding</code></li><li><code>cellspacing</code></li><li><code>width</code></li><li><code>height</code></li></ul><p>For <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>:</p><ul><li><code>align</code></li><li><code>valign</code></li></ul><h2 id="colspan-103"><code>colspan</code></h2><p>Merges multiple columns into one cell.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;th colspan=&quot;3&quot;&gt;A&lt;/th&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="rowspan-104"><code>rowspan</code></h2><p>Merges multiple rows into one cell.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;th rowspan=&quot;3&quot;&gt;A&lt;/th&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="table-attribute-summary-105">Table attribute summary</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th><code>&lt;table&gt;</code></th><th><code>&lt;tr&gt;</code></th><th><code>&lt;td&gt;/&lt;th&gt;</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>border</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>bgcolor</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>align</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>width</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>height</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>colspan</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>rowspan</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>cellspacing</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>cellpadding</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-27-images-106"><h1 id="27-images-106">27. Images</h1><p>The <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag embeds images.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;img src=&quot;image.jpg&quot;
     alt=&quot;Description&quot;
     width=&quot;500&quot;
     height=&quot;300&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>Important attributes:</p><ul><li><code>src</code> – image location</li><li><code>alt</code> – alternative text</li><li><code>width</code></li><li><code>height</code></li><li><code>title</code></li><li><code>border</code></li></ul><p>The source notes that changing both width and height can distort an image. Maintain the aspect ratio where appropriate.</p><p>The image path may be:</p><ul><li>relative URL</li><li>absolute URL</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-28-audio-107"><h1 id="28-audio-107">28. Audio</h1><p>The <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code> tag embeds audio content.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;audio src=&quot;audio.mp3&quot; controls&gt;
&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre><p>Multiple formats can be provided using <code>&lt;source&gt;</code>:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;audio controls&gt;
    &lt;source src=&quot;audio.mp3&quot; type=&quot;audio/mp3&quot;&gt;
    &lt;source src=&quot;audio.ogg&quot; type=&quot;audio/ogg&quot;&gt;
&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre><p>Important attributes:</p><ul><li><code>src</code></li><li><code>controls</code></li><li><code>autoplay</code></li><li><code>loop</code></li><li><code>muted</code></li><li><code>preload</code></li></ul><p><code>preload</code> values:</p><ul><li><code>auto</code></li><li><code>metadata</code></li><li><code>none</code></li></ul><p><code>&lt;source&gt;</code> attributes:</p><ul><li><code>src</code></li><li><code>type</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-29-video-108"><h1 id="29-video-108">29. Video</h1><p>The <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> tag embeds video.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;video src=&quot;video.mp4&quot; controls&gt;
&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre><p>Multiple formats can be supplied with nested <code>&lt;source&gt;</code> tags.</p><p>Attributes described:</p><ul><li><code>src</code></li><li><code>controls</code></li><li><code>width</code></li><li><code>height</code></li><li><code>autoplay</code></li><li><code>loop</code></li><li><code>muted</code></li><li><code>preload</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-30-embed-tag-109"><h1 id="30-embed-tag-109">30. <code>&lt;embed&gt;</code> Tag</h1><p>The <code>&lt;embed&gt;</code> tag can embed external content such as:</p><ul><li>audio</li><li>video</li><li>PDF</li><li>images</li><li>other external resources</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;embed src=&quot;file.pdf&quot; width=&quot;500&quot; height=&quot;400&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>Main attributes:</p><ul><li><code>src</code></li><li><code>width</code></li><li><code>height</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-31-hyperlinks-110"><h1 id="31-hyperlinks-110">31. Hyperlinks</h1><p>Hyperlinks connect one resource to another.</p><p>They allow users to:</p><ul><li>move from one webpage to another</li><li>move within the same webpage</li><li>access external resources</li></ul><p>When a user hovers over a hyperlink, the pointer normally changes to indicate that the element is clickable.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-32-anchor-tag-111"><h1 id="32-anchor-tag-111">32. Anchor Tag</h1><p>The <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag creates hyperlinks.</p><p>Important attributes:</p><h2 id="href-112"><code>href</code></h2><p>Specifies the destination.</p><h2 id="target-113"><code>target</code></h2><p>Determines where the linked document opens.</p><h3 id="self-114"><code>_self</code></h3><p>Default. Opens in the same window/tab.</p><h3 id="blank-115"><code>_blank</code></h3><p>Opens in a new window/tab.</p><h2 id="title-116"><code>title</code></h2><p>Provides additional information as a tooltip.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.example.com&quot;
   target=&quot;_blank&quot;
   title=&quot;Visit Example&quot;&gt;
    Visit Example
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-33-uses-of-the-anchor-tag-117"><h1 id="33-uses-of-the-anchor-tag-117">33. Uses of the Anchor Tag</h1><h2 id="external-link-118">External link</h2><p>Links to a website or online resource.</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.example.com&quot;&gt;
    Visit External Site
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="link-to-a-specific-external-resource-119">Link to a specific external resource</h2><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.example.com/video.mp4&quot;&gt;
    Watch Video
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="link-to-a-local-multimedia-file-120">Link to a local multimedia file</h2><p>Can point to:</p><ul><li>video</li><li>image</li><li>audio</li><li>PDF</li><li>other local files</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-34-absolute-and-relative-path-names-121"><h1 id="34-absolute-and-relative-path-names-121">34. Absolute and Relative Path Names</h1><h2 id="absolute-path-122">Absolute Path</h2><p>Shows the complete location from the root.</p><p>Examples:</p><pre><code class="language-text">/home/user/Documents/report.txt
C:\Users\user\Documents\report.txt</code></pre><h2 id="relative-path-123">Relative Path</h2><p>Shows a file location relative to the current working directory.</p><p>Examples:</p><pre><code class="language-text">xyz.jpg
\images\cheems.jpg</code></pre><p>Relative paths are usually shorter.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-35-image-or-button-as-a-link-124"><h1 id="35-image-or-button-as-a-link-124">35. Image or Button as a Link</h1><p>An image can be placed inside an anchor:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com&quot;&gt;
    &lt;img src=&quot;bird.jpg&quot;&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><p>A button can also be placed inside an anchor:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com&quot;&gt;
    &lt;button&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-36-linking-multiple-pages-of-the-same-website-125"><h1 id="36-linking-multiple-pages-of-the-same-website-125">36. Linking Multiple Pages of the Same Website</h1><p>A website normally consists of interconnected webpages.</p><p>The home page acts as a starting point and provides links to other pages.</p><p>Example:</p><p><code>home.html</code></p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;h3&gt;This is the home page&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;a href=&quot;contact.html&quot;&gt;
    Go to Contact Us
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><p><code>contact.html</code></p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;h3&gt;This is the contact us page&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;a href=&quot;home.html&quot;&gt;
    Go to Home
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-37-bookmarks-same-page-navigation-126"><h1 id="37-bookmarks-same-page-navigation-126">37. Bookmarks / Same-Page Navigation</h1><p>Bookmarks allow users to jump to a particular section of the same webpage.</p><h2 id="step-1-create-an-id-127">Step 1 – Create an ID</h2><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;h2 id=&quot;section1&quot;&gt;Section 1&lt;/h2&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="step-2-link-to-the-id-128">Step 2 – Link to the ID</h2><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;a href=&quot;#section1&quot;&gt;
    Go to Section 1
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><p>The <code>#</code> identifies the page fragment.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-38-center-tag-129"><h1 id="38-center-tag-129">38. <code>&lt;center&gt;</code> Tag</h1><p>The <code>&lt;center&gt;</code> tag centers content horizontally.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;center&gt;
    &lt;h2&gt;Title&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;img src=&quot;image.jpg&quot;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;This content is centered.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/center&gt;</code></pre><p>The source notes that <code>&lt;center&gt;</code> is deprecated in HTML5 and CSS should be used instead.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-39-marquee-tag-130"><h1 id="39-marquee-tag-130">39. <code>&lt;marquee&gt;</code> Tag</h1><p>The source describes <code>&lt;marquee&gt;</code> as a way to create automatic scrolling content.</p><p>It can scroll:</p><ul><li>text</li><li>images</li><li>links</li><li>inline content</li></ul><p>Attributes described:</p><ul><li><code>direction</code></li><li><code>behavior</code></li><li><code>scrollamount</code></li><li><code>scrolldelay</code></li><li><code>loop</code></li><li><code>width</code></li><li><code>height</code></li></ul><p>Behavior values:</p><ul><li><code>scroll</code></li><li><code>slide</code></li><li><code>alternate</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-part-02-css-131"><h1 id="part-02-css-131">PART 02 – CSS</h1></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-40-css-cascading-style-sheets-132"><h1 id="40-css-cascading-style-sheets-132">40. CSS – Cascading Style Sheets</h1><p>CSS is a stylesheet language used to control the visual presentation of webpages.</p><p>HTML:</p><ul><li>structure</li><li>content</li></ul><p>CSS:</p><ul><li>presentation</li><li>appearance</li><li>layout</li></ul><p>CSS can control:</p><ul><li>text colors</li><li>background colors</li><li>fonts</li><li>spacing</li><li>alignment</li><li>layout</li><li>element sizes</li><li>positioning</li><li>borders</li><li>shadows</li><li>animations</li><li>responsive behavior</li></ul><p>CSS also separates presentation from HTML structure and supports consistent styling across multiple pages.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-41-css-development-timeline-133"><h1 id="41-css-development-timeline-133">41. CSS Development Timeline</h1><p>The source gives this timeline:</p><h3 id="19941996-origins-of-css-134">1994–1996 – Origins of CSS</h3><ul><li>Proposed by Håkon Wium Lie in 1994.</li><li>CSS1 released by W3C in 1996.</li><li>Basic styling included colors, fonts and spacing.</li></ul><h3 id="1998-css2-135">1998 – CSS2</h3><ul><li>Media types</li><li>positioning</li><li>box model</li></ul><h3 id="2004-css2-1-136">2004 – CSS2.1</h3><ul><li>clarified/fixed CSS2 issues</li><li>improved browser consistency</li><li>became widely adopted</li></ul><h3 id="2011-css3-137">2011 – CSS3</h3><ul><li>modular approach</li><li>animations</li><li>transitions</li><li>gradients</li></ul><h3 id="post-2011-modern-css-138">Post-2011 – Modern CSS</h3><ul><li>Flexbox</li><li>CSS Grid</li><li>responsive/complex layouts</li><li>CSS Variables</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-42-ways-of-inserting-css-139"><h1 id="42-ways-of-inserting-css-139">42. Ways of Inserting CSS</h1><p>There are three main methods.</p><h2 id="1-inline-css-140">1. Inline CSS</h2><p>Style is written directly inside an HTML element using the <code>style</code> attribute.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;p style=&quot;color:red;&quot;&gt;Hello&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre><p>The source notes:</p><ul><li>inline CSS has high priority</li><li>it can override internal/external CSS</li><li>it is discouraged for maintainability and scalability when used extensively</li></ul><h2 id="2-internal-css-141">2. Internal CSS</h2><p>CSS is placed inside a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;head&gt;
&lt;style&gt;
body {
    background-color: red;
}

p {
    font-size: 10px;
    color: navy;
}
&lt;/style&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;</code></pre><p>Useful when:</p><ul><li>styles are specific to one page</li><li>working with a single-page application</li></ul><h2 id="3-external-css-142">3. External CSS</h2><p>Styles are stored in a separate <code>.css</code> file.</p><p>The HTML document links the stylesheet:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;
      type=&quot;text/css&quot;
      href=&quot;styles.css&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>Important attributes:</p><ul><li><code>rel="stylesheet"</code></li><li><code>type="text/css"</code></li><li><code>href="..."</code></li></ul><h3 id="import-143"><code>@import</code></h3><p>The source also describes <code>@import</code> for including an external stylesheet.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">@import url(&quot;styles/main.css&quot;);</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-43-css-priority-144"><h1 id="43-css-priority-144">43. CSS Priority</h1><p>When multiple CSS rules target the same element, CSS uses a priority system.</p><p>According to the source:</p><ol><li>Inline CSS – highest among the listed normal sources</li><li>Internal/External CSS – same level; when both apply, the later declaration takes precedence</li><li>Browser default styles – lowest</li></ol><p>More specific selectors can override less-specific styles.</p><h2 id="important-145"><code>!important</code></h2><p>The <code>!important</code> rule gives a declaration higher priority and can override other declarations.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    color: red !important;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-44-css-syntax-146"><h1 id="44-css-syntax-146">44. CSS Syntax</h1><p>Basic syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-css">selector {
    property: value;
}</code></pre><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 16px;
}</code></pre><h3 id="selector-147">Selector</h3><p>Identifies the HTML element(s) to style.</p><h3 id="declaration-block-148">Declaration block</h3><p>Enclosed in <code>{ }</code>.</p><h3 id="declaration-149">Declaration</h3><p>Contains:</p><ul><li>property</li><li>value</li></ul><p>The property and value are separated by <code>:</code> and declarations end with <code>;</code>.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-45-css-selectors-150"><h1 id="45-css-selectors-150">45. CSS Selectors</h1><h2 id="1-element-selector-151">1. Element Selector</h2><p>Targets all elements of a particular type.</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    font-size: 20px;
    color: red;
}</code></pre><h2 id="2-id-selector-152">2. ID Selector</h2><p>Targets an element with a specific ID.</p><pre><code class="language-css">#header {
    font-size: 24px;
    color: crimson;
}</code></pre><p>HTML:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;h1 id=&quot;header&quot;&gt;Heading&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre><p>An ID identifies a particular element.</p><h2 id="3-class-selector-153">3. Class Selector</h2><p>Targets elements with a particular class.</p><pre><code class="language-css">.highlight {
    background-color: yellow;
    font-weight: bold;
}</code></pre><p>HTML:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;p class=&quot;highlight&quot;&gt;Text&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1 class=&quot;highlight&quot;&gt;Heading&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="4-universal-selector-154">4. Universal Selector</h2><p>Targets all elements.</p><pre><code class="language-css">* {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    box-sizing: border-box;
}</code></pre><h2 id="5-group-selector-155">5. Group Selector</h2><p>Applies the same style to multiple selectors.</p><pre><code class="language-css">h1, p, .highlight, #unique {
    color: blue;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}</code></pre><p>Selectors are separated using commas.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-46-compound-selectors-156"><h1 id="46-compound-selectors-156">46. Compound Selectors</h1><p>Compound selectors combine selectors without spaces.</p><h2 id="element-class-157">Element + Class</h2><pre><code class="language-css">p.highlight {
    color: red;
}</code></pre><p>Targets only <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> elements having class <code>highlight</code>.</p><h2 id="element-id-158">Element + ID</h2><pre><code class="language-css">h1#title {
    font-size: 32px;
}</code></pre><p>Targets an <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> whose ID is <code>title</code>.</p><h2 id="multiple-class-selector-159">Multiple Class Selector</h2><pre><code class="language-css">.btn.primary {
    padding: 10px;
}</code></pre><p>Targets elements containing both classes.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-47-attribute-selectors-160"><h1 id="47-attribute-selectors-160">47. Attribute Selectors</h1><p>Attribute selectors target elements based on attributes.</p><p>Forms described in the source:</p><h3 id="attr-161"><code>[attr]</code></h3><p>Targets elements having a particular attribute.</p><h3 id="attr-value-162"><code>[attr="value"]</code></h3><p>Targets an exact attribute value.</p><h3 id="attr-value-163"><code>[attr^="value"]</code></h3><p>Targets attributes beginning with a value.</p><h3 id="attr-value-164"><code>[attr$="value"]</code></h3><p>Targets attributes ending with a value.</p><h3 id="attr-value-165"><code>[attr*="value"]</code></h3><p>Targets attributes containing a value.</p><p>Examples:</p><pre><code class="language-css">[href^=&quot;https&quot;] {
    color: green;
}

[src$=&quot;.png&quot;] {
    border: 2px solid red;
}

[title] {
    font-weight: bold;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-48-css-appearance-formatting-166"><h1 id="48-css-appearance-formatting-166">48. CSS Appearance Formatting</h1><h2 id="font-properties-167">Font Properties</h2><h3 id="font-style-168"><code>font-style</code></h3><p>Controls normal/italic/oblique style.</p><pre><code class="language-css">em {
    font-style: italic;
}</code></pre><h3 id="font-variant-169"><code>font-variant</code></h3><p>Controls small-caps.</p><h3 id="font-weight-170"><code>font-weight</code></h3><p>Controls thickness.</p><p>Possible examples:</p><ul><li>normal</li><li>bold</li><li>lighter</li><li>numeric values such as 100–900</li></ul><h3 id="font-size-171"><code>font-size</code></h3><p>Controls text size.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li><code>16px</code></li><li><code>1.5rem</code></li><li><code>%</code></li><li><code>em</code></li></ul><h3 id="line-height-172"><code>line-height</code></h3><p>Controls line spacing.</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    line-height: 1.5;
}</code></pre><h3 id="font-family-173"><code>font-family</code></h3><p>Specifies the font.</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-49-css-shorthand-properties-174"><h1 id="49-css-shorthand-properties-174">49. CSS Shorthand Properties</h1><p>Shorthand allows several related CSS properties to be written in one declaration.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    text-decoration: underline dotted red 2px;
}</code></pre><p>Equivalent longhand:</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-style: dotted;
    text-decoration-color: red;
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
}</code></pre><p>Important rule:</p><ul><li>shorthand can be convenient</li><li>the order of values matters</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-50-text-properties-175"><h1 id="50-text-properties-175">50. Text Properties</h1><h2 id="color-176"><code>color</code></h2><p>Changes text color.</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    color: #333333;
}</code></pre><h2 id="text-align-177"><code>text-align</code></h2><p>Controls horizontal alignment:</p><ul><li>left</li><li>right</li><li>center</li><li>justify</li></ul><h2 id="text-transform-178"><code>text-transform</code></h2><p>Controls capitalization.</p><p>Values:</p><ul><li><code>none</code></li><li><code>capitalize</code></li><li><code>uppercase</code></li><li><code>lowercase</code></li><li><code>full-width</code></li></ul><h2 id="text-indent-179"><code>text-indent</code></h2><p>Controls indentation of the first line.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">p {
    text-indent: 2em;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-51-text-shadow-180"><h1 id="51-text-shadow-180">51. Text Shadow</h1><p>Adds a shadow to text.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-text">text-shadow: h-offset v-offset blur-radius color;</code></pre><h3 id="horizontal-offset-181">Horizontal offset</h3><ul><li>positive → right</li><li>negative → left</li></ul><h3 id="vertical-offset-182">Vertical offset</h3><ul><li>positive → downward</li><li>negative → upward</li></ul><h3 id="blur-radius-183">Blur radius</h3><p>Controls the amount of blur.</p><h3 id="color-184">Color</h3><p>Controls shadow color.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">h2 {
    text-shadow: 2px 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-52-text-decoration-185"><h1 id="52-text-decoration-185">52. Text Decoration</h1><h2 id="text-decoration-line-186"><code>text-decoration-line</code></h2><p>Values:</p><ul><li><code>none</code></li><li><code>underline</code></li><li><code>overline</code></li><li><code>line-through</code></li></ul><p>Multiple values can be combined.</p><h2 id="text-decoration-color-187"><code>text-decoration-color</code></h2><p>Sets decoration color.</p><h2 id="text-decoration-style-188"><code>text-decoration-style</code></h2><p>Values include:</p><ul><li>solid</li><li>double</li><li>dotted</li><li>dashed</li><li>wavy</li></ul><h2 id="text-decoration-thickness-189"><code>text-decoration-thickness</code></h2><p>Controls line thickness.</p><p>Possible values include:</p><ul><li>specific length such as <code>2px</code></li><li><code>auto</code></li><li><code>from-font</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-53-link-states-190"><h1 id="53-link-states-190">53. Link States</h1><p>CSS can style different states of links.</p><h2 id="a-link-191"><code>a:link</code></h2><p>Unvisited link.</p><h2 id="a-visited-192"><code>a:visited</code></h2><p>Visited link.</p><h2 id="a-hover-193"><code>a:hover</code></h2><p>Pointer is over the link.</p><h2 id="a-active-194"><code>a:active</code></h2><p>Link is being clicked.</p><h2 id="a-focus-195"><code>a:focus</code></h2><p>Link has keyboard focus.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">a:hover {
    color: red;
    text-decoration: underline;
}</code></pre><h2 id="lvha-order-196">LVHA order</h2><p>The source gives the order:</p><ol><li><code>:link</code></li><li><code>:visited</code></li><li><code>:hover</code></li><li><code>:active</code></li></ol><p>Focus is also described separately.</p><p>To remove default link styling:</p><pre><code class="language-css">a {
    text-decoration: none;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-54-css-list-properties-197"><h1 id="54-css-list-properties-197">54. CSS List Properties</h1><h2 id="list-style-type-198"><code>list-style-type</code></h2><p>Controls list markers.</p><p>For unordered lists:</p><ul><li>disc</li><li>circle</li><li>square</li><li>none</li></ul><p>For ordered lists:</p><ul><li>decimal</li><li>decimal-leading-zero</li><li>lower-roman</li><li>upper-roman</li><li>lower-alpha</li><li>upper-alpha</li><li>lower-greek</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">ol {
    list-style-type: upper-roman;
}</code></pre><h2 id="list-style-position-199"><code>list-style-position</code></h2><p>Controls marker position:</p><ul><li><code>outside</code></li><li><code>inside</code></li></ul><h2 id="list-style-image-200"><code>list-style-image</code></h2><p>Allows a custom image to be used as a marker.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">ul {
    list-style-image: url(&#x27;custom-bullet.png&#x27;);
}</code></pre><h2 id="reset-list-styles-201">Reset list styles</h2><pre><code class="language-css">ul, ol {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
}</code></pre><h2 id="list-style-shorthand-202"><code>list-style</code> shorthand</h2><p>Combines:</p><ul><li>list-style-type</li><li>list-style-position</li><li>list-style-image</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">ul {
    list-style: square inside url(&#x27;bullet.png&#x27;);
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-55-css-spacing-essentials-203"><h1 id="55-css-spacing-essentials-203">55. CSS Spacing Essentials</h1><h2 id="letter-spacing-204">Letter spacing</h2><p>Controls space between characters.</p><pre><code class="language-css">letter-spacing: 2px;</code></pre><h2 id="word-spacing-205">Word spacing</h2><p>Controls space between words.</p><pre><code class="language-css">word-spacing: 10px;</code></pre><h2 id="line-spacing-206">Line spacing</h2><p>Controlled using <code>line-height</code>.</p><h2 id="text-indentation-207">Text indentation</h2><p>Controlled using <code>text-indent</code>.</p><h2 id="border-spacing-208">Border spacing</h2><p>Used with tables.</p><h2 id="white-space-209">White space</h2><p>Controls how spaces, tabs and line breaks are handled.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">white-space: nowrap;</code></pre><p>This prevents text from wrapping.</p><h2 id="none-as-a-css-value-210"><code>none</code> as a CSS value</h2><p><code>none</code> can be used to remove default or existing styles.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">a {
    text-decoration: none;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-56-css-backgrounds-211"><h1 id="56-css-backgrounds-211">56. CSS Backgrounds</h1><p>The <code>background</code> property is a shorthand property.</p><p>Related properties described:</p><ul><li><code>background-color</code></li><li><code>background-image</code></li><li><code>background-position</code></li><li><code>background-size</code></li><li><code>background-repeat</code></li><li><code>background-attachment</code></li><li><code>background-origin</code></li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">background-color: #f0f0f0;
background-image: url(&#x27;background.jpg&#x27;);
background-position: center center;
background-size: cover;
background-repeat: no-repeat;
background-attachment: fixed;
background-origin: padding-box;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-57-css-comments-212"><h1 id="57-css-comments-212">57. CSS Comments</h1><p>CSS comments begin with:</p><pre><code class="language-css">/*</code></pre><p>and end with:</p><pre><code class="language-css">*/</code></pre><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">/* This is a comment */</code></pre><p>Multi-line:</p><pre><code class="language-css">/*
This is a
multi-line comment.
*/</code></pre><p>Browsers ignore CSS comments.</p><p>Uses:</p><ul><li>explain code</li><li>document code</li><li>temporarily disable styles during development</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-58-css-box-model-213"><h1 id="58-css-box-model-213">58. CSS Box Model</h1><p>The CSS Box Model describes the rectangular areas generated around webpage elements.</p><p>It consists of:</p><h2 id="1-content-214">1. Content</h2><p>The actual element content:</p><ul><li>text</li><li>images</li><li>etc.</li></ul><p><code>width</code> and <code>height</code> define the content area.</p><h2 id="2-padding-215">2. Padding</h2><p>Space between content and border.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">padding: 10px;</code></pre><h2 id="3-border-216">3. Border</h2><p>Surrounds the padding/content.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">border: 1px solid black;</code></pre><h2 id="4-margin-217">4. Margin</h2><p>Space outside the border separating the element from other elements.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">margin: 20px;</code></pre><h2 id="5-border-radius-218">5. Border Radius</h2><p>Rounds the corners.</p><pre><code class="language-css">border-radius: 25px;</code></pre><h2 id="6-box-shadow-219">6. Box Shadow</h2><p>Adds a shadow around the element.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">box-shadow: 2px 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-59-css-borders-220"><h1 id="59-css-borders-220">59. CSS Borders</h1><p>Borders define the boundary/outline of an element.</p><h2 id="border-width-221"><code>border-width</code></h2><p>Controls thickness.</p><p>Values can include:</p><ul><li>thin</li><li>medium</li><li>thick</li><li>specific lengths</li></ul><h2 id="border-style-222"><code>border-style</code></h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>none</li><li>solid</li><li>dashed</li><li>dotted</li><li>double</li><li>groove</li><li>ridge</li><li>inset</li><li>outset</li></ul><h2 id="border-color-223"><code>border-color</code></h2><p>Can use:</p><ul><li>named color</li><li>hexadecimal</li><li>RGB</li><li>HSL</li></ul><h2 id="border-radius-224"><code>border-radius</code></h2><p>Rounds corners.</p><h2 id="border-225"><code>border</code></h2><p>Shorthand for:</p><ul><li>width</li><li>style</li><li>color</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-css">border: 2px solid black;</code></pre><h2 id="border-image-226"><code>border-image</code></h2><p>Allows an image to be used as a border.</p><p>Border sides can also be styled separately:</p><pre><code class="language-css">border-top: 4px solid green;
border-right: 2px dotted red;
border-bottom: 5px dashed blue;
border-left: 1px double black;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-60-css-table-properties-227"><h1 id="60-css-table-properties-227">60. CSS Table Properties</h1><h2 id="width-and-height-228"><code>width</code> and <code>height</code></h2><p>Control table or element dimensions.</p><h2 id="border-229"><code>border</code></h2><p>Adds borders.</p><h2 id="border-collapse-230"><code>border-collapse</code></h2><p>Controls whether adjacent borders are combined.</p><p>Values:</p><ul><li><code>collapse</code></li><li><code>separate</code></li></ul><h2 id="border-spacing-231"><code>border-spacing</code></h2><p>Controls spacing between cells when borders are separate.</p><h2 id="text-align-232"><code>text-align</code></h2><p>Horizontal alignment:</p><ul><li>left</li><li>center</li><li>right</li><li>justify</li></ul><h2 id="vertical-align-233"><code>vertical-align</code></h2><p>Vertical alignment:</p><ul><li>top</li><li>middle</li><li>bottom</li></ul><h2 id="padding-234"><code>padding</code></h2><p>Creates space inside table cells.</p><h2 id="caption-side-235"><code>caption-side</code></h2><p>Positions the table caption:</p><ul><li>top</li><li>bottom</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-61-css-validation-236"><h1 id="61-css-validation-236">61. CSS Validation</h1><p>A CSS validator checks whether CSS is correctly written.</p><p>It helps identify:</p><ul><li>syntax errors</li><li>unsupported selectors</li><li>unsupported properties</li><li>unsupported values</li></ul><p>The source identifies the W3C CSS validation service: <code>http://jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator</code></p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-62-css-measuring-units-237"><h1 id="62-css-measuring-units-237">62. CSS Measuring Units</h1><p>CSS provides units for:</p><ul><li>size</li><li>distance</li><li>position</li></ul><h2 id="relative-units-238">Relative Units</h2><p>Relative units depend on context such as:</p><ul><li>parent element</li><li>viewport</li><li>font size</li></ul><p>They are useful for responsive designs.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>Percentage of parent value</td></tr><tr><td><code>em</code></td><td>Relative to element font size</td></tr><tr><td><code>rem</code></td><td>Relative to root <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> font size</td></tr><tr><td><code>vw</code></td><td>1% of viewport width</td></tr><tr><td><code>vh</code></td><td>1% of viewport height</td></tr><tr><td><code>vmin</code></td><td>Smaller of <code>vw</code> and <code>vh</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>vmax</code></td><td>Larger of <code>vw</code> and <code>vh</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ch</code></td><td>Width of the <code>0</code> character</td></tr><tr><td><code>ex</code></td><td>Height of lowercase <code>x</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>cap</code></td><td>Height of capital letters</td></tr><tr><td><code>lh</code></td><td>Current line-height</td></tr><tr><td><code>ic</code></td><td>Size of the East Asian “水” character</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="absolute-units-239">Absolute Units</h2><p>Fixed units are not affected by other elements or screen sizes.</p><p>Useful for:</p><ul><li>print</li><li>fixed layouts</li><li>situations requiring precision</li></ul><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>px</td><td>Pixel</td></tr><tr><td>cm</td><td>Centimeter</td></tr><tr><td>mm</td><td>Millimeter</td></tr><tr><td>in</td><td>Inch; 1 inch = 2.54 cm</td></tr><tr><td>pt</td><td>Point; 1/72 inch</td></tr><tr><td>pc</td><td>Pica; 1 pica = 12 points</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-63-web-authoring-tools-240"><h1 id="63-web-authoring-tools-240">63. Web Authoring Tools</h1><p>A web authoring tool helps create, design and publish web content.</p><p>Instead of manually writing all HTML/CSS/JavaScript, some tools provide visual interfaces such as drag-and-drop editors that generate code.</p><h2 id="purposes-241">Purposes</h2><h3 id="simplify-website-creation-242">Simplify website creation</h3><p>Makes website creation easier for beginners/non-technical users.</p><h3 id="speed-up-development-243">Speed up development</h3><p>Visual editors and templates can create pages faster.</p><h3 id="reduce-errors-244">Reduce errors</h3><p>Automatically generated code can reduce common syntax and formatting mistakes.</p><h3 id="provide-professional-results-245">Provide professional results</h3><p>Modern tools may provide:</p><ul><li>responsive layouts</li><li>SEO tools</li><li>e-commerce options</li><li>analytics</li><li>other integrated features</li></ul><h3 id="collaboration-246">Collaboration</h3><p>Some tools support:</p><ul><li>team editing</li><li>version control</li><li>workflows</li></ul><h2 id="types-of-web-authoring-tools-247">Types of Web Authoring Tools</h2><h3 id="wysiwyg-html-editors-248">WYSIWYG HTML Editors</h3><p>“What You See Is What You Get”</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Adobe Dreamweaver</li><li>BlueGriffon</li><li>Webflow</li><li>Google Web Designer</li></ul><h3 id="source-code-editors-249">Source Code Editors</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Visual Studio Code</li><li>Sublime Text</li><li>Atom</li><li>Notepad++</li></ul><h3 id="conversion-tools-250">Conversion Tools</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Markdown-to-HTML tools</li><li>Grammarly HTML Formatter</li><li>HTTrack Website Copier</li></ul><h3 id="debugging-tools-251">Debugging Tools</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Visual Studio Debugger</li><li>Lighthouse</li><li>Charles Proxy</li></ul><h3 id="integrated-development-environments-ides-252">Integrated Development Environments (IDEs)</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Eclipse for Web Developers</li><li>JetBrains PhpStorm</li><li>NetBeans</li></ul><h3 id="template-based-generators-253">Template-Based Generators</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Tilda</li><li>Carrd</li><li>Webflow Templates</li></ul><h3 id="content-management-systems-cms-254">Content Management Systems (CMS)</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Wix</li><li>Weebly</li><li>Shopify</li></ul><h3 id="email-clients-designers-255">Email Clients/Designers</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Mailchimp Email Designer</li><li>Postbox</li><li>Campaign Monitor</li></ul><h3 id="multimedia-authoring-tools-256">Multimedia Authoring Tools</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Adobe Animate</li><li>Unity WebGL Export</li><li>Kapwing</li></ul><h3 id="mobile-web-app-creators-257">Mobile Web App Creators</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Thunkable</li><li>AppGyver</li><li>Ionic Framework</li></ul><h3 id="scripting-libraries-and-frameworks-258">Scripting Libraries and Frameworks</h3><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>jQuery UI</li><li>Vue.js</li><li>Meteo</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-64-web-hosting-259"><h1 id="64-web-hosting-259">64. Web Hosting</h1><p>Web hosting is a service that provides the infrastructure, technologies and resources needed to store, manage and deliver website data over the Internet.</p><h2 id="core-components-260">Core components</h2><h3 id="website-261">Website</h3><p>Collection of webpages and resources.</p><h3 id="web-server-262">Web Server</h3><p>Computer/software that stores the website and delivers it to users.</p><h3 id="hosting-provider-263">Hosting Provider</h3><p>Organization that owns, manages and maintains web servers and rents server space.</p><h3 id="domain-name-264">Domain Name</h3><p>Human-readable address mapped to a server's IP address.</p><h3 id="24-7-power-and-internet-connectivity-265">24/7 power and Internet connectivity</h3><p>Helps keep websites continuously accessible.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-65-web-hosting-operation-266"><h1 id="65-web-hosting-operation-266">65. Web Hosting Operation</h1><p>A simplified flow:</p><ol><li>User enters a domain name.</li><li>DNS resolves the domain name to an IP address.</li><li>Browser sends an HTTP/HTTPS request.</li><li>Web server processes the request.</li><li>Website content is sent back.</li><li>Browser renders the website.</li></ol><h3 id="dns-step-267">DNS step</h3><p>DNS translates the domain name into the IP address of the web server.</p><h3 id="http-https-request-268">HTTP/HTTPS request</h3><p>The browser requests website resources.</p><p>HTTPS provides secure communication between browser and server.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-66-hosting-resources-269"><h1 id="66-hosting-resources-269">66. Hosting Resources</h1><h2 id="disk-space-270">Disk Space</h2><p>Stores:</p><ul><li>webpages</li><li>images</li><li>videos</li><li>databases</li></ul><h2 id="bandwidth-271">Bandwidth</h2><p>Amount of data that can be transferred between server and users.</p><p>Higher bandwidth can support more simultaneous visitors.</p><h2 id="cpu-272">CPU</h2><p>Provides processing power for:</p><ul><li>website requests</li><li>server-side tasks</li></ul><h2 id="ram-273">RAM</h2><p>Helps manage:</p><ul><li>active processes</li><li>multiple users</li><li>high traffic</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-67-types-of-web-hosting-274"><h1 id="67-types-of-web-hosting-274">67. Types of Web Hosting</h1><h2 id="1-shared-hosting-275">1. Shared Hosting</h2><ul><li>Multiple websites share one server.</li><li>Lower cost.</li><li>Limited resources/performance.</li></ul><h2 id="2-vps-hosting-276">2. VPS Hosting</h2><p>Virtual Private Server.</p><ul><li>One physical server is divided into virtual servers.</li><li>Allocated resources.</li><li>Better performance/control than shared hosting.</li></ul><h2 id="3-dedicated-hosting-277">3. Dedicated Hosting</h2><ul><li>One website uses an entire physical server.</li><li>High performance.</li><li>Maximum control.</li><li>Improved security.</li></ul><h2 id="4-cloud-hosting-278">4. Cloud Hosting</h2><ul><li>Website hosted across multiple interconnected servers.</li><li>Supports scalability, reliability and availability.</li></ul><h2 id="5-managed-hosting-279">5. Managed Hosting</h2><p>The hosting provider handles:</p><ul><li>server maintenance</li><li>updates</li><li>security</li></ul><p>This reduces technical work for the user.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-68-advantages-of-web-hosting-280"><h1 id="68-advantages-of-web-hosting-280">68. Advantages of Web Hosting</h1><p>Web hosting:</p><ul><li>makes websites accessible globally 24/7</li><li>provides stable servers and high uptime</li><li>provides disk space, bandwidth, CPU and RAM</li><li>may provide SSL, firewalls and malware protection</li><li>provides technical support</li><li>allows resource scaling as traffic increases</li><li>supports backups and recovery</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-69-static-vs-dynamic-hosting-281"><h1 id="69-static-vs-dynamic-hosting-281">69. Static vs Dynamic Hosting</h1><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Static Hosting</th><th>Dynamic Hosting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Content</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Changes based on input/database</td></tr><tr><td>Technologies</td><td>HTML, CSS, basic JavaScript</td><td>PHP, Python, Node.js, ASP.NET with databases</td></tr><tr><td>Server processing</td><td>Minimal</td><td>Required</td></tr><tr><td>Database</td><td>Not required</td><td>Often used</td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Very fast/simple delivery</td><td>Some processing overhead</td></tr><tr><td>Security</td><td>Fewer vulnerabilities in the source comparison</td><td>Requires stronger security</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher due to resource use</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Easier</td><td>Regular updates/maintenance</td></tr><tr><td>Scalability</td><td>Limited functionality</td><td>Highly scalable/flexible</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Portfolio sites, landing pages</td><td>E-commerce, social media platforms</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-70-local-publishing-282"><h1 id="70-local-publishing-282">70. Local Publishing</h1><p>Local publishing means creating and viewing a website on your own computer before making it available on the Internet.</p><p>Developers can test locally before uploading.</p><h2 id="local-server-software-283">Local server software</h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>XAMPP</li><li>WAMP</li><li>MAMP</li><li>localhost servers</li></ul><p>These simulate a web server on the local computer.</p><p>Local publishing helps to:</p><ul><li>check design</li><li>fix errors</li><li>test forms</li><li>test databases</li><li>improve performance</li></ul><h2 id="steps-284">Steps</h2><ol><li>Install local server software such as XAMPP.</li><li>Start the server.</li><li>Create a project folder.</li><li>Open the <code>htdocs</code> folder.</li><li>Create a website folder, for example:<br><code>htdocs/mywebsite</code></li><li>Add website files.</li><li>Open the browser.</li><li>Visit:<br><code>http://localhost/mywebsite</code></li><li>Test the website.</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-71-internet-publishing-285"><h1 id="71-internet-publishing-285">71. Internet Publishing</h1><p>Internet publishing means uploading website files to a web hosting server so users around the world can access the website.</p><h2 id="steps-286">Steps</h2><ol><li>Create and fully test the website locally.</li><li>Register/buy a domain name.</li><li>Purchase web hosting.</li><li>Log into the hosting account.</li><li>Open the control panel, such as cPanel.</li><li>Open File Manager.</li><li>Locate <code>public_html</code>.</li><li>Upload website files.</li><li>Make sure the homepage is named:<ul><li><code>index.html</code></li><li>or <code>index.php</code></li></ul></li><li>If a database is required:<ul><li>create/import the database</li><li>update configuration/database details</li></ul></li><li>Enter the domain name in a browser.</li><li>Check that the website works correctly online.</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-72-factors-affecting-website-performance-287"><h1 id="72-factors-affecting-website-performance-287">72. Factors Affecting Website Performance</h1><h2 id="1-bandwidth-288">1. Bandwidth</h2><p>Low bandwidth can slow downloading, especially with many users.</p><h2 id="2-web-hosting-quality-289">2. Web Hosting Quality</h2><p>Poor or shared hosting can reduce speed and reliability.</p><h2 id="3-server-speed-cpu-ram-290">3. Server Speed – CPU &amp; RAM</h2><p>Low processing power can slow server responses.</p><h2 id="4-server-response-time-291">4. Server Response Time</h2><p>Time taken by the server to respond to a browser request.</p><h2 id="5-network-latency-292">5. Network Latency</h2><p>Delay caused by physical distance between user and server.</p><h2 id="6-website-file-size-293">6. Website File Size</h2><p>Large:</p><ul><li>images</li><li>videos</li><li>graphics</li></ul><p>can increase loading time.</p><h2 id="7-number-of-http-requests-294">7. Number of HTTP Requests</h2><p>More:</p><ul><li>scripts</li><li>images</li><li>stylesheets</li></ul><p>can mean more requests and slower loading.</p><h2 id="8-unoptimized-images-and-media-295">8. Unoptimized Images and Media</h2><p>Poorly compressed media can reduce performance.</p><h2 id="9-poor-coding-296">9. Poor Coding</h2><p>Inefficient:</p><ul><li>HTML</li><li>CSS</li><li>JavaScript</li></ul><p>can slow a website.</p><h2 id="10-too-many-plugins-or-external-scripts-297">10. Too Many Plugins or External Scripts</h2><p>Excessive plugins, especially in CMS platforms, can affect speed.</p><h2 id="11-database-performance-298">11. Database Performance</h2><p>Slow or poorly structured database queries can delay dynamic pages.</p><h2 id="12-traffic-volume-299">12. Traffic Volume</h2><p>A high number of simultaneous visitors can overload a server.</p><h2 id="13-lack-of-caching-300">13. Lack of Caching</h2><p>Without caching, content may be reloaded completely on every visit.</p><h2 id="14-server-location-301">14. Server Location</h2><p>A server far from users can increase loading time.</p><h2 id="15-user-internet-speed-302">15. User Internet Speed</h2><p>A slow Internet connection affects how quickly webpage content loads.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-part-03-php-303"><h1 id="part-03-php-303">PART 03 – PHP</h1></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-73-introduction-to-php-304"><h1 id="73-introduction-to-php-304">73. Introduction to PHP</h1><p>PHP is a server-side scripting language widely used for web development.</p><p>Originally, PHP stood for <strong>Personal Home Page</strong>.</p><h3 id="history-from-the-source-305">History from the source</h3><p><strong>1994</strong></p><ul><li>Created by Rasmus Lerdorf.</li><li>Initially consisted of Perl scripts for managing his personal website and tracking visits.</li></ul><p><strong>1995 – PHP/FI 1.0</strong></p><ul><li>Rewritten in C.</li><li>Provided web form processing and database communication.</li></ul><p><strong>PHP 3.0 – 1998</strong></p><ul><li>Modular architecture.</li><li>Expanded database support.</li></ul><p><strong>PHP 4.0 – 2000</strong></p><ul><li>Powered by Zend Engine.</li><li>Performance improvements.</li><li>Sessions and output buffering.</li></ul><p><strong>PHP 5.0 – 2004</strong></p><ul><li>Better object-oriented programming support.</li><li>Classes</li><li>interfaces</li><li>exceptions</li><li>improved XML handling</li></ul><p><strong>PHP 7.0 – 2015</strong></p><ul><li>Major performance improvements.</li><li>Scalar type declarations.</li><li>Return type declarations.</li><li>Better error handling.</li></ul><p><strong>PHP 8.0 – 2020</strong></p><ul><li>JIT compiler.</li><li>Union types.</li><li>Named arguments.</li><li>Match expressions.</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-74-static-vs-dynamic-webpages-306"><h1 id="74-static-vs-dynamic-webpages-306">74. Static vs Dynamic Webpages</h1><h2 id="static-webpages-307">Static Webpages</h2><p>Static webpages:</p><ul><li>display fixed content</li><li>are commonly built using HTML, CSS and sometimes JavaScript</li><li>remain the same unless the developer manually changes the code</li></ul><h2 id="dynamic-webpages-308">Dynamic Webpages</h2><p>Dynamic webpages:</p><ul><li>can change based on user interaction, preferences or other factors</li><li>can use server-side languages such as PHP, Ruby, Python or Node.js</li><li>often interact with databases</li></ul><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Static</th><th>Dynamic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Content</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Changes</td></tr><tr><td>Generation</td><td>Pre-generated HTML</td><td>Server-side generation</td></tr><tr><td>Complexity</td><td>Simple</td><td>More complex</td></tr><tr><td>Interactivity</td><td>Limited</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Fast</td><td>Slower due to processing</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Manual updates</td><td>Backend/automatic updates</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Wikipedia/portfolio-style static pages</td><td>E-commerce/social/media services</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-75-how-php-works-309"><h1 id="75-how-php-works-309">75. How PHP Works</h1><p>PHP follows a server-side processing model.</p><h3 id="step-1-request-310">Step 1 – Request</h3><p>A browser requests a <code>.php</code> page from a web server.</p><h3 id="step-2-php-processing-311">Step 2 – PHP Processing</h3><p>The web server sends the PHP file to the PHP interpreter.</p><h3 id="step-3-database-interaction-312">Step 3 – Database Interaction</h3><p>If required, PHP communicates with a database such as MySQL.</p><h3 id="step-4-dynamic-content-generation-313">Step 4 – Dynamic Content Generation</h3><p>PHP uses program logic and retrieved data to generate dynamic content.</p><h3 id="step-5-html-output-314">Step 5 – HTML Output</h3><p>The PHP interpreter converts the processed PHP code into HTML output.</p><h3 id="step-6-server-response-315">Step 6 – Server Response</h3><p>The server sends the generated HTML back to the browser.</p><h3 id="step-7-client-display-316">Step 7 – Client Display</h3><p>The browser renders the HTML and displays the final webpage.</p><h3 id="flow-317">Flow</h3><pre><code class="language-text">Browser
   ↓
Web Server
   ↓
PHP Interpreter
   ↓
Database (if needed)
   ↓
Generated HTML
   ↓
Web Server
   ↓
Browser</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-76-client-side-vs-server-side-processing-318"><h1 id="76-client-side-vs-server-side-processing-318">76. Client-Side vs Server-Side Processing</h1><h2 id="client-side-processing-319">Client-side processing</h2><ul><li>HTML and CSS are processed on the client's computer through the browser.</li><li>The client's CPU and memory are used.</li><li>The browser parses and renders the webpage.</li></ul><h2 id="server-side-processing-320">Server-side processing</h2><ul><li>PHP executes on the server before content is sent to the client.</li><li>The server's CPU, memory and power are used.</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-77-uses-of-php-321"><h1 id="77-uses-of-php-321">77. Uses of PHP</h1><p>The source identifies PHP use in:</p><h2 id="content-management-systems-322">Content Management Systems</h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>WordPress</li><li>Joomla</li><li>Drupal</li></ul><h2 id="e-commerce-323">E-commerce</h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Magento</li><li>WooCommerce</li></ul><h2 id="frameworks-324">Frameworks</h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Laravel</li><li>Symfony</li><li>CodeIgniter</li></ul><h2 id="social-media-platforms-325">Social Media Platforms</h2><p>PHP has been used for backend services/API responses in platforms such as Facebook and parts of Twitter and LinkedIn.</p><h2 id="online-forums-326">Online Forums</h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>phpBB</li><li>vBulletin</li></ul><h2 id="web-services-and-apis-327">Web Services and APIs</h2><p>PHP can be used to create RESTful APIs and web services.</p><h2 id="custom-web-applications-328">Custom Web Applications</h2><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>CRM systems</li><li>inventory management systems</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-78-virtual-server-329"><h1 id="78-virtual-server-329">78. Virtual Server</h1><p>PHP runs on a server, so PHP scripts cannot simply be executed directly like an ordinary HTML file.</p><p>A server environment is needed to:</p><ul><li>process PHP</li><li>handle HTTP requests</li></ul><p>A developer can use a virtual/local server on their own computer for testing.</p><p>Common server packages:</p><ul><li>WAMP</li><li>XAMPP</li><li>MAMP</li><li>LAMP</li></ul><p>These packages combine components such as:</p><ul><li>Apache web server</li><li>MySQL/MariaDB database server</li><li>PHP</li><li>sometimes Perl/Python depending on the package</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-79-wamp-mamp-lamp-and-xampp-330"><h1 id="79-wamp-mamp-lamp-and-xampp-330">79. WAMP, MAMP, LAMP and XAMPP</h1><h2 id="wamp-331">WAMP</h2><p>Designed for Windows.</p><p>WAMP combines:</p><ul><li>Windows</li><li>Apache</li><li>MySQL</li><li>PHP</li></ul><h2 id="mamp-332">MAMP</h2><p>Designed for macOS.</p><p>Combines:</p><ul><li>macOS</li><li>Apache</li><li>MySQL/MariaDB</li><li>PHP</li></ul><h2 id="lamp-333">LAMP</h2><p>Commonly used on Linux.</p><p>Combines:</p><ul><li>Linux</li><li>Apache</li><li>MySQL/MariaDB</li><li>PHP</li></ul><h2 id="xampp-334">XAMPP</h2><p>Cross-platform package supporting:</p><ul><li>Windows</li><li>macOS</li><li>Linux</li></ul><p>Includes:</p><ul><li>Apache</li><li>MySQL/MariaDB</li><li>PHP</li><li>optionally Perl</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-80-setting-up-wamp-335"><h1 id="80-setting-up-wamp-335">80. Setting Up WAMP</h1><p>The source demonstrates:</p><ol><li>Download WAMP.</li><li>Run the installer.</li><li>Follow installation instructions.</li><li>Find the WAMP shortcut.</li><li>Launch WAMP.</li><li>Open a browser.</li><li>Enter:<br><code>http://localhost/</code></li><li>The WAMP homepage should appear if the server is running correctly.</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-81-setting-up-xampp-336"><h1 id="81-setting-up-xampp-336">81. Setting Up XAMPP</h1><ol><li>Download the appropriate version for the operating system.</li><li>Run the installer.</li><li>Select required components, normally:<ul><li>Apache</li><li>MySQL</li><li>PHP</li></ul></li><li>Complete installation.</li><li>Open XAMPP Control Panel.</li><li>Start Apache and MySQL.</li><li>Open the <code>htdocs</code> directory.</li><li>Create a project folder.</li><li>Create a PHP file such as <code>index.php</code>.</li><li>Example:</li></ol><pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php
echo &quot;Hello, world!&quot;;
?&gt;</code></pre><ol><li>Open:</li></ol><pre><code class="language-text">http://localhost/my_project/index.php</code></pre><p>The browser should display:</p><pre><code class="language-text">Hello, world!</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-82-wamp-project-folder-337"><h1 id="82-wamp-project-folder-337">82. WAMP Project Folder</h1><p>The source explains that WAMP normally uses:</p><ul><li><code>C:\wamp64\</code> on 64-bit systems</li><li><code>C:\wamp\</code> on 32-bit systems</li></ul><p>The <code>www</code> folder is used for web projects.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-text">C:\wamp64\www\myproject</code></pre><p>A PHP file can be created inside the project folder.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-text">C:\wamp64\www\myproject\index.php</code></pre><p>The browser can then access:</p><pre><code class="language-text">http://localhost/myproject/index.php</code></pre><p>A <code>.php</code> file can contain:</p><ul><li>HTML</li><li>CSS</li><li>PHP</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-83-php-syntax-338"><h1 id="83-php-syntax-338">83. PHP Syntax</h1><p>PHP code is normally written between:</p><pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php
    // PHP code
?&gt;</code></pre><p>PHP can be embedded inside HTML.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;body&gt;

&lt;?php
echo &quot;Hello World!&quot;;
?&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="semicolon-339">Semicolon</h2><p>A semicolon <code>;</code> indicates the end of a PHP instruction.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-84-echo-and-print-340"><h1 id="84-echo-and-print-340">84. <code>echo</code> and <code>print</code></h1><p>Both are used to output data.</p><h2 id="echo-341"><code>echo</code></h2><ul><li>language construct</li><li>can take multiple parameters</li><li>no return value</li><li>source describes it as slightly faster than <code>print</code></li></ul><h2 id="print-342"><code>print</code></h2><ul><li>language construct</li><li>takes one argument</li><li>returns <code>1</code></li><li>can therefore be used in expressions</li><li>source describes it as slightly slower than <code>echo</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-85-php-variables-343"><h1 id="85-php-variables-343">85. PHP Variables</h1><p>Variables store data that can be used and changed in a program.</p><p>PHP variables begin with <code>$</code>.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$name = &quot;John&quot;;
$age = 20;</code></pre><h2 id="important-rules-344">Important rules</h2><ul><li>begin with <code>$</code></li><li>variable name must start with a letter or underscore</li><li>cannot start with a number</li><li>can contain letters, numbers and underscores</li><li>cannot use reserved words</li><li>variable names are case-sensitive</li><li>PHP does not require explicit declaration of variable type</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$variable
$Variable</code></pre><p>These are different variables.</p><p>The PHP language itself is not case-sensitive for commands such as:</p><pre><code class="language-text">Echo
echo
eCHo</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-86-php-comments-345"><h1 id="86-php-comments-345">86. PHP Comments</h1><h2 id="single-line-comment-using-346">Single-line comment using <code>//</code></h2><pre><code class="language-php">// This is a comment</code></pre><h2 id="single-line-comment-using-347">Single-line comment using <code>#</code></h2><pre><code class="language-php"># This is a comment</code></pre><h2 id="multi-line-comment-348">Multi-line comment</h2><pre><code class="language-php">/*
This is a
multi-line comment
*/</code></pre><p>Uses:</p><ul><li>readability</li><li>debugging</li><li>maintenance</li><li>collaboration</li><li>documentation</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-87-php-arithmetic-operators-349"><h1 id="87-php-arithmetic-operators-349">87. PHP Arithmetic Operators</h1><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>Addition</td></tr><tr><td><code>-</code></td><td>Subtraction</td></tr><tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>Multiplication</td></tr><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>Division</td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>Modulus/remainder</td></tr><tr><td><code>**</code></td><td>Exponentiation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">echo 10 + 5;
echo 10 - 5;
echo 10 * 5;
echo 10 / 5;
echo 11 % 5;
echo 5 ** 2;</code></pre><p>Important note from the source:</p><ul><li>Python-style <code>//</code> integer division is <strong>not</strong> used as a PHP arithmetic operator in this lesson.</li><li>In PHP, <code>//</code> begins a single-line comment.</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-88-assignment-operators-350"><h1 id="88-assignment-operators-350">88. Assignment Operators</h1><h2 id="basic-assignment-351">Basic assignment</h2><pre><code class="language-php">$a = 10;</code></pre><p>The right-side value is assigned to the left-side variable.</p><h2 id="augmented-assignment-352">Augmented assignment</h2><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$a = 10;
$a += 5;</code></pre><p>Result:</p><pre><code class="language-text">15</code></pre><p>The source notes that augmented assignment can also be used with other arithmetic operators.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-89-logical-operators-353"><h1 id="89-logical-operators-353">89. Logical Operators</h1><p>Used to combine conditions.</p><h2 id="and-354">AND</h2><pre><code class="language-php">&amp;&amp;
and</code></pre><p>True when both operands are true.</p><h2 id="or-355">OR</h2><pre><code class="language-php">||
or</code></pre><p>True when at least one operand is true.</p><h2 id="not-356">NOT</h2><pre><code class="language-php">!</code></pre><p>Reverses the logical state.</p><h2 id="xor-357">XOR</h2><pre><code class="language-php">xor</code></pre><p>True when one operand is true but not both.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-90-comparison-operators-358"><h1 id="90-comparison-operators-358">90. Comparison Operators</h1><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>==</code></td><td>Equal</td></tr><tr><td><code>===</code></td><td>Identical – equal value and same type</td></tr><tr><td><code>!=</code></td><td>Not equal</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Not equal</td></tr><tr><td><code>!==</code></td><td>Not identical</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;</code></td><td>Less than</td></tr><tr><td><code>&gt;</code></td><td>Greater than</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;=</code></td><td>Less than or equal</td></tr><tr><td><code>&gt;=</code></td><td>Greater than or equal</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Important distinction:</p><pre><code class="language-php">20 == 20</code></pre><p>checks equality.</p><pre><code class="language-php">20 === 20</code></pre><p>checks equality <strong>and type</strong>.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-91-increment-and-decrement-359"><h1 id="91-increment-and-decrement-359">91. Increment and Decrement</h1><h2 id="increment-360">Increment <code>++</code></h2><p>Increases a variable by one.</p><h3 id="pre-increment-361">Pre-increment</h3><pre><code class="language-php">++$number</code></pre><p>The variable is increased first, then the new value is used.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$number = 5;
echo ++$number;</code></pre><p>Output:</p><pre><code class="language-text">6</code></pre><h3 id="post-increment-362">Post-increment</h3><pre><code class="language-php">$number++</code></pre><p>The current value is used first, then the variable increases.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$number = 5;
echo $number++;</code></pre><p>Output:</p><pre><code class="language-text">5</code></pre><p>Afterwards the variable is <code>6</code>.</p><h2 id="decrement-363">Decrement <code>--</code></h2><p>Decreases by one.</p><h3 id="pre-decrement-364">Pre-decrement</h3><pre><code class="language-php">--$number</code></pre><h3 id="post-decrement-365">Post-decrement</h3><pre><code class="language-php">$number--</code></pre><p>The same pre/post principle applies.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-92-vardump-366"><h1 id="92-vardump-366">92. <code>var_dump()</code></h1><p><code>var_dump()</code> displays structured information about variables.</p><p>It can show:</p><ul><li>data type</li><li>value</li><li>additional details such as string length</li></ul><p>Useful for debugging.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$number = 42;
$string = &quot;Hello, World!&quot;;
$float = 3.14;
$boolean = true;

var_dump($number);
var_dump($string);
var_dump($float);
var_dump($boolean);</code></pre><p>Possible output contains:</p><ul><li><code>int</code></li><li><code>string</code></li><li><code>float</code></li><li><code>boolean</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-93-php-data-types-367"><h1 id="93-php-data-types-367">93. PHP Data Types</h1><h2 id="string-368">String</h2><p>Sequence of characters in single or double quotation marks.</p><pre><code class="language-php">$name = &quot;John&quot;;</code></pre><h2 id="integer-369">Integer</h2><p>Non-decimal number.</p><pre><code class="language-php">$number = 42;</code></pre><h2 id="float-double-370">Float / Double</h2><p>Number with a decimal point.</p><pre><code class="language-php">$floatNumber = 3.14;</code></pre><h2 id="boolean-371">Boolean</h2><p>Two states:</p><ul><li><code>true</code></li><li><code>false</code></li></ul><h2 id="array-372">Array</h2><p>Collection of values accessed using an index or key.</p><p>Three main types:</p><ol><li>indexed arrays</li><li>associative arrays</li><li>multidimensional arrays</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-94-indexed-arrays-373"><h1 id="94-indexed-arrays-373">94. Indexed Arrays</h1><p>Use numeric indexes.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$indexedArray = array(10, &quot;apple&quot;, 3.14, true);</code></pre><p>Indexes:</p><pre><code class="language-text">0
1
2
3</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-95-associative-arrays-374"><h1 id="95-associative-arrays-374">95. Associative Arrays</h1><p>Use named keys.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$assocArray = array(
    &quot;a&quot; =&gt; 10,
    &quot;b&quot; =&gt; &quot;apple&quot;,
    &quot;c&quot; =&gt; 3.14,
    &quot;d&quot; =&gt; true
);</code></pre><p>The keys are:</p><ul><li><code>a</code></li><li><code>b</code></li><li><code>c</code></li><li><code>d</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-96-multidimensional-arrays-375"><h1 id="96-multidimensional-arrays-375">96. Multidimensional Arrays</h1><p>An array can contain one or more arrays.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$multiArray = array(
    &quot;nums&quot; =&gt; array(10, 20, 30),
    &quot;letters&quot; =&gt; array(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;)
);</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-97-if-statement-376"><h1 id="97-if-statement-376">97. <code>if</code> Statement</h1><p>Used to execute a block when a condition is true.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">if (condition) {
    // code
}</code></pre><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$age = 20;

if ($age &gt;= 18) {
    echo &quot;You are eligible to vote.&quot;;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-98-if-else-377"><h1 id="98-if-else-377">98. <code>if-else</code></h1><p>Executes one block if true and another if false.</p><pre><code class="language-php">if (condition) {
    // true
} else {
    // false
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-99-if-elseif-else-378"><h1 id="99-if-elseif-else-378">99. <code>if-elseif-else</code></h1><p>Used to test multiple conditions.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">if ($score &gt;= 90) {
    echo &quot;Grade: A&quot;;
} elseif ($score &gt;= 80) {
    echo &quot;Grade: B&quot;;
} elseif ($score &gt;= 70) {
    echo &quot;Grade: C&quot;;
} else {
    echo &quot;Grade: F&quot;;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-100-nested-if-379"><h1 id="100-nested-if-379">100. Nested <code>if</code></h1><p>An <code>if</code> statement can be placed inside another <code>if</code>.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">if ($age &gt;= 18) {
    if ($hasLicense == true) {
        echo &quot;You can drive the car.&quot;;
    }
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-101-ternary-operator-380"><h1 id="101-ternary-operator-380">101. Ternary Operator</h1><p>The shorthand form of if-else.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">condition ? true_value : false_value;</code></pre><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$age = 20;

$message = ($age &gt;= 18)
    ? &quot;You are an adult.&quot;
    : &quot;You are a minor.&quot;;

echo $message;</code></pre><p>The source also demonstrates nested ternary operators, while noting that excessive nesting can reduce readability.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-102-switch-statement-381"><h1 id="102-switch-statement-381">102. Switch Statement</h1><p>Used when different actions depend on different possible values.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">switch ($variable) {
    case value1:
        // code
        break;

    case value2:
        // code
        break;

    default:
        // code
}</code></pre><h2 id="important-parts-382">Important parts</h2><h3 id="switch-383"><code>switch</code></h3><p>Starts the selection structure.</p><h3 id="case-384"><code>case</code></h3><p>Specifies a possible value.</p><h3 id="break-385"><code>break</code></h3><p>Ends the current case.</p><p>Without <code>break</code>, execution may continue into following cases. This is called <strong>fall-through</strong>.</p><h3 id="default-386"><code>default</code></h3><p>Optional block executed when no case matches.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-103-loops-in-php-387"><h1 id="103-loops-in-php-387">103. Loops in PHP</h1><p>Loops repeat a block of code.</p><p>Four main loop types:</p><ol><li><code>for</code></li><li><code>foreach</code></li><li><code>while</code></li><li><code>do-while</code></li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-104-for-loop-388"><h1 id="104-for-loop-388">104. <code>for</code> Loop</h1><p>Used when the number of repetitions is known in advance.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">for (initialization; condition; increment) {
    // code
}</code></pre><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">for ($x = 0; $x &lt;= 10; $x++) {
    echo &quot;The number is: $x &lt;br&gt;&quot;;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-105-while-loop-389"><h1 id="105-while-loop-389">105. <code>while</code> Loop</h1><p>Runs while the condition remains true.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">while (condition) {
    // code
}</code></pre><p>The number of repetitions can be controlled by:</p><ol><li>changing the control variable</li><li>changing the condition</li><li>changing the increment/decrement</li></ol><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$x = 1;

while ($x &lt;= 5) {
    echo &quot;The number is: $x &lt;br&gt;&quot;;
    $x++;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-106-do-while-loop-390"><h1 id="106-do-while-loop-390">106. <code>do-while</code> Loop</h1><p>A <code>do-while</code> loop:</p><ol><li>executes the block first</li><li>checks the condition afterwards</li></ol><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">do {
    // code
} while (condition);</code></pre><p>Therefore, it executes at least once.</p><h2 id="while-vs-do-while-391">while vs do-while</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>while</th><th>do-while</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Condition check</td><td>Before execution</td><td>After execution</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum executions</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Best use</td><td>When execution depends completely on condition</td><td>When code must run at least once</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-107-foreach-loop-392"><h1 id="107-foreach-loop-392">107. <code>foreach</code> Loop</h1><p>Used to loop through arrays.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">foreach ($array as $value) {
    // code
}</code></pre><p>For associative arrays:</p><pre><code class="language-php">foreach ($ages as $name =&gt; $age) {
    echo &quot;$name is $age years old.&quot;;
}</code></pre><p>The loop processes each array element until the last element.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-108-nested-loops-393"><h1 id="108-nested-loops-393">108. Nested Loops</h1><p>A loop can be placed inside another loop.</p><p>Useful for:</p><ul><li>multidimensional arrays</li><li>complex iterations</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">for ($i = 1; $i &lt;= 3; $i++) {
    for ($j = 1; $j &lt;= 3; $j++) {
        echo &quot;$i - $j&quot;;
    }
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-109-break-394"><h1 id="109-break-394">109. <code>break</code></h1><p>Immediately exits a loop.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">for ($x = 0; $x &lt; 10; $x++) {
    if ($x == 5) {
        break;
    }
    echo $x;
}</code></pre><p>The loop stops when <code>$x</code> becomes 5.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-110-continue-395"><h1 id="110-continue-395">110. <code>continue</code></h1><p>Skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">for ($x = 0; $x &lt; 7; $x++) {
    if ($x == 5) {
        continue;
    }

    echo $x;
}</code></pre><p>The value 5 is skipped.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-111-php-functions-396"><h1 id="111-php-functions-396">111. PHP Functions</h1><p>A function is a block of code designed to perform a specific task.</p><p>Functions help with:</p><ul><li>code organization</li><li>reuse</li><li>readability</li><li>maintainability</li></ul><h2 id="defining-a-function-397">Defining a function</h2><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">function functionName() {
    // code
}</code></pre><h2 id="calling-a-function-398">Calling a function</h2><p>Write the function name followed by parentheses.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">function greet() {
    echo &quot;Hello, world!&quot;;
}

greet();</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-112-four-function-categories-399"><h1 id="112-four-function-categories-399">112. Four Function Categories</h1><h2 id="1-parameters-no-return-value-400">1. Parameters, no return value</h2><p>Accepts input but does not return a value.</p><pre><code class="language-php">function greet($name) {
    echo &quot;Hello, $name!&quot;;
}</code></pre><h2 id="2-no-parameters-return-value-401">2. No parameters, return value</h2><p>Accepts no input but returns a result.</p><pre><code class="language-php">function getGreeting() {
    return &quot;Hello, world!&quot;;
}</code></pre><h2 id="3-parameters-and-return-value-402">3. Parameters and return value</h2><p>Accepts input and returns a result.</p><pre><code class="language-php">function add($a, $b) {
    return $a + $b;
}</code></pre><h2 id="4-no-parameters-and-no-return-value-403">4. No parameters and no return value</h2><p>Neither accepts input nor returns a result.</p><pre><code class="language-php">function greet() {
    echo &quot;Hello, world!&quot;;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-113-variable-scope-404"><h1 id="113-variable-scope-404">113. Variable Scope</h1><h2 id="local-variables-405">Local Variables</h2><p>Variables declared inside a function have local scope.</p><p>They can normally be accessed only inside that function.</p><h2 id="global-variables-406">Global Variables</h2><p>Variables declared outside functions have global scope.</p><p>They cannot normally be accessed directly inside a function unless explicitly made available.</p><p>A global <code>$x</code> and a local <code>$x</code> are treated as different variables.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-114-global-keyword-407"><h1 id="114-global-keyword-407">114. <code>global</code> Keyword</h1><p>The <code>global</code> keyword can be used inside a function to access a global variable.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$globalVar = &quot;I&#x27;m a global variable&quot;;

function testScope() {
    global $globalVar;
    echo $globalVar;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-115-globals-array-408"><h1 id="115-globals-array-408">115. <code>$GLOBALS</code> Array</h1><p><code>$GLOBALS</code> is a superglobal associative array containing references to variables in the global scope.</p><p>It can be used from anywhere in the script, including functions.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$globalVar = &quot;I&#x27;m a global variable&quot;;

function testGlobalAccess() {
    echo $GLOBALS[&#x27;globalVar&#x27;];
}</code></pre><p>The keys are the names of global variables and the values are their contents.</p><p>It can also be used to modify global variables.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-116-default-function-parameters-409"><h1 id="116-default-function-parameters-409">116. Default Function Parameters</h1><p>A function parameter can have a default value.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">function greet($name = &quot;Guest&quot;) {
    echo &quot;Hello, $name!&quot;;
}

greet();
greet(&quot;Alice&quot;);</code></pre><p>If no argument is supplied, the default value is used.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-117-common-built-in-functions-410"><h1 id="117-common-built-in-functions-410">117. Common Built-in Functions</h1><h2 id="string-functions-411">String Functions</h2><h3 id="strlen-412"><code>strlen()</code></h3><p>Returns the length of a string.</p><pre><code class="language-php">strlen(&quot;Hello&quot;);</code></pre><p>Result:</p><pre><code class="language-text">5</code></pre><h3 id="strreplace-413"><code>str_replace()</code></h3><p>Replaces occurrences of a string.</p><pre><code class="language-php">str_replace(&quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;Hi&quot;, $txt);</code></pre><h3 id="substr-414"><code>substr()</code></h3><p>Returns part of a string.</p><pre><code class="language-php">substr(&quot;Hello world!&quot;, 6, 5);</code></pre><p>Result:</p><pre><code class="language-text">world</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-118-string-operators-415"><h1 id="118-string-operators-415">118. String Operators</h1><h2 id="concatenation-416">Concatenation <code>.</code></h2><p>Joins strings.</p><pre><code class="language-php">$fullName = $firstName . $lastName;</code></pre><h2 id="concatenation-assignment-417">Concatenation assignment <code>.=</code></h2><p>Appends the right-hand string.</p><pre><code class="language-php">$fullName = &quot;John&quot;;
$fullName .= &quot; Hamilton&quot;;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-119-array-functions-418"><h1 id="119-array-functions-418">119. Array Functions</h1><h2 id="count-419"><code>count()</code></h2><p>Counts elements.</p><h2 id="arraymerge-420"><code>array_merge()</code></h2><p>Merges arrays.</p><h2 id="arraypush-421"><code>array_push()</code></h2><p>Adds elements to the end of an array.</p><h2 id="arraykeys-422"><code>array_keys()</code></h2><p>Returns array keys.</p><h2 id="sort-423"><code>sort()</code></h2><p>Sorts an array in ascending order.</p><h2 id="rsort-424"><code>rsort()</code></h2><p>Sorts an array in descending order.</p><h2 id="asort-425"><code>asort()</code></h2><p>Sorts an associative array by value in ascending order.</p><h2 id="ksort-426"><code>ksort()</code></h2><p>Sorts an associative array by key in ascending order.</p><h2 id="arsort-427"><code>arsort()</code></h2><p>Sorts an associative array by value in descending order.</p><h2 id="krsort-428"><code>krsort()</code></h2><p>Sorts an associative array by key in descending order.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-120-mathematical-functions-429"><h1 id="120-mathematical-functions-429">120. Mathematical Functions</h1><h2 id="abs-430"><code>abs()</code></h2><p>Returns absolute value.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">abs(-4.2)</code></pre><p>Result:</p><pre><code class="language-text">4.2</code></pre><h2 id="round-431"><code>round()</code></h2><p>Rounds a floating-point number.</p><pre><code class="language-php">round(3.6)</code></pre><h2 id="rand-432"><code>rand()</code></h2><p>Generates a random integer.</p><pre><code class="language-php">rand(1, 10)</code></pre><h2 id="sqrt-433"><code>sqrt()</code></h2><p>Returns square root.</p><pre><code class="language-php">sqrt(16)</code></pre><p>Result:</p><pre><code class="language-text">4</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-121-other-useful-php-functions-434"><h1 id="121-other-useful-php-functions-434">121. Other Useful PHP Functions</h1><h2 id="isset-435"><code>isset()</code></h2><p>Checks whether a variable exists and is not <code>NULL</code>.</p><h2 id="empty-436"><code>empty()</code></h2><p>Checks whether a variable is empty.</p><h2 id="printr-437"><code>print_r()</code></h2><p>Prints human-readable information about a variable, especially useful for arrays.</p><h2 id="die-exit-438"><code>die()</code> / <code>exit()</code></h2><p>Displays a message and terminates the current script.</p><h2 id="implode-439"><code>implode()</code></h2><p>Joins array elements into one string.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">implode(separator, array);</code></pre><h2 id="explode-440"><code>explode()</code></h2><p>Splits a string into an array using a delimiter.</p><p>Syntax:</p><pre><code class="language-php">explode(separator, string);</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-122-static-keyword-441"><h1 id="122-static-keyword-441">122. <code>static</code> Keyword</h1><p>The <code>static</code> keyword allows a local variable inside a function to retain its value between function calls.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">function counter() {
    static $count = 0;
    $count++;
    echo $count;
}

counter();
counter();
counter();</code></pre><p>Output:</p><pre><code class="language-text">1
2
3</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-123-nested-ternary-operators-442"><h1 id="123-nested-ternary-operators-442">123. Nested Ternary Operators</h1><p>Ternary operators can be nested.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$score = 85;

$grade = ($score &gt;= 90) ? &quot;A&quot; :
         (($score &gt;= 80) ? &quot;B&quot; :
         (($score &gt;= 70) ? &quot;C&quot; : &quot;F&quot;));</code></pre><p>The source notes that nesting can reduce readability.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-124-html-forms-with-php-443"><h1 id="124-html-forms-with-php-443">124. HTML Forms with PHP</h1><p>HTML forms collect user input and send it to a server for processing.</p><p>Forms are important for:</p><ul><li>login</li><li>searching</li><li>data entry</li><li>interactive web applications</li></ul><h2 id="benefits-444">Benefits</h2><h3 id="access-control-445">Access Control</h3><p>Forms act as an intermediary between users and the database.</p><h3 id="validation-446">Validation</h3><p>Input can be checked and sanitized.</p><h3 id="consistent-data-entry-447">Consistent Data Entry</h3><p>Forms can enforce formats and data types.</p><h3 id="error-checking-448">Error Checking</h3><p>Forms can provide feedback for incorrect/incomplete data.</p><h3 id="user-experience-449">User Experience</h3><p>Forms can use:</p><ul><li>dropdowns</li><li>checkboxes</li><li>radio buttons</li><li>other input types</li></ul><h3 id="controlled-data-flow-450">Controlled Data Flow</h3><p>Forms allow developers to control how data is:</p><ul><li>submitted</li><li>processed</li><li>stored</li></ul><h3 id="data-preprocessing-451">Data Preprocessing</h3><p>Data can be formatted/processed before storage.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-125-basic-html-form-structure-452"><h1 id="125-basic-html-form-structure-452">125. Basic HTML Form Structure</h1><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;form action=&quot;submit.php&quot;
      method=&quot;post&quot;
      autocomplete=&quot;on&quot;&gt;

    &lt;!-- form elements --&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="form-attributes-453"><code>&lt;form&gt;</code> attributes</h2><h3 id="action-454"><code>action</code></h3><p>Specifies where form data is sent.</p><h3 id="method-455"><code>method</code></h3><p>Specifies the HTTP method:</p><ul><li>GET</li><li>POST</li></ul><h3 id="autocomplete-456"><code>autocomplete</code></h3><p>Controls whether browser autocomplete is enabled.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-126-input-element-457"><h1 id="126-input-element-457">126. <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> Element</h1><p>The <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> element is widely used for form controls.</p><p>Important attributes:</p><ul><li><code>id</code></li><li><code>type</code></li><li><code>value</code></li><li><code>placeholder</code></li><li><code>readonly</code></li><li><code>required</code></li><li><code>disabled</code></li><li><code>name</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-127-name-attribute-458"><h1 id="127-name-attribute-458">127. <code>name</code> Attribute</h1><p>The <code>name</code> attribute is extremely important in form submission.</p><p>When a form is submitted, the browser sends data as key-value pairs.</p><p>The <code>name</code> attribute becomes the key.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot;
       name=&quot;username&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>If the user enters <code>johndoe</code>, the submitted pair contains:</p><pre><code class="language-text">username=johndoe</code></pre><p>The server-side PHP code uses the name to retrieve the value.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-128-label-tag-459"><h1 id="128-label-tag-459">128. <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> Tag</h1><p>The <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> element provides a label for an input.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;label for=&quot;username&quot;&gt;Username:&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot;
       id=&quot;username&quot;
       name=&quot;u_name&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>The <code>for</code> attribute connects the label to the input's <code>id</code>.</p><p>Clicking the label can focus/select the related input.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-129-fieldset-and-legend-460"><h1 id="129-fieldset-and-legend-460">129. <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code></h1><h2 id="fieldset-461"><code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code></h2><p>Groups related form elements.</p><h2 id="legend-462"><code>&lt;legend&gt;</code></h2><p>Provides a caption/title for the fieldset.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;fieldset&gt;
    &lt;legend&gt;Personal Information&lt;/legend&gt;

    &lt;label for=&quot;name&quot;&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot;
           id=&quot;name&quot;
           name=&quot;name&quot;&gt;
&lt;/fieldset&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-130-html-input-types-463"><h1 id="130-html-input-types-463">130. HTML Input Types</h1><p>The source covers:</p><ol><li><code>text</code> – single-line text</li><li><code>password</code> – password input, hides characters</li><li><code>email</code> – email input with validation</li><li><code>number</code> – numeric input</li><li><code>date</code> – date picker</li><li><code>time</code> – time picker</li><li><code>checkbox</code> – multiple independent selections</li><li><code>radio</code> – one selection from a group</li><li><code>file</code> – file upload</li><li><code>hidden</code> – hidden data submitted with the form</li><li><code>submit</code> – submit form</li><li><code>reset</code> – reset form</li><li><code>color</code> – color picker</li><li><code>range</code> – slider for a numeric range</li><li><code>url</code> – URL input with validation</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-131-checkboxes-464"><h1 id="131-checkboxes-464">131. Checkboxes</h1><p>Checkboxes allow multiple options.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot;
       name=&quot;preferences[]&quot;
       value=&quot;newsletter&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>Using:</p><pre><code class="language-text">preferences[]</code></pre><p>allows selected values to be submitted as an array.</p><p>PHP can process the array using <code>foreach</code>.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-132-radio-buttons-465"><h1 id="132-radio-buttons-465">132. Radio Buttons</h1><p>Radio buttons allow one option from a group.</p><p>Radio buttons are grouped by giving them the same <code>name</code>.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot;
       name=&quot;gender&quot;
       value=&quot;male&quot;&gt;

&lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot;
       name=&quot;gender&quot;
       value=&quot;female&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>Only the selected value is submitted.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-133-select-and-option-466"><h1 id="133-select-and-option-466">133. <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;option&gt;</code></h1><p>Used to create dropdown lists.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;select name=&quot;vehicle&quot; id=&quot;vehicle-select&quot;&gt;
    &lt;option value=&quot;car&quot;&gt;Car&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;option value=&quot;bike&quot;&gt;Bike&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;</code></pre><h2 id="select-attributes-467"><code>&lt;select&gt;</code> attributes</h2><ul><li><code>name</code></li><li><code>id</code></li><li><code>size</code></li><li><code>multiple</code></li><li><code>disabled</code></li></ul><h2 id="option-attributes-468"><code>&lt;option&gt;</code> attributes</h2><ul><li><code>value</code></li><li><code>selected</code></li><li><code>disabled</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-134-textarea-469"><h1 id="134-textarea-469">134. <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code></h1><p>Creates a multi-line text input.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;textarea
    id=&quot;message&quot;
    name=&quot;message&quot;
    rows=&quot;5&quot;
    cols=&quot;30&quot;&gt;
&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre><p>Attributes:</p><ul><li><code>id</code></li><li><code>rows</code></li><li><code>cols</code></li><li><code>name</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-135-form-action-470"><h1 id="135-form-action-470">135. Form <code>action</code></h1><p>The <code>action</code> attribute identifies the URL/script that processes submitted data.</p><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;form action=&quot;process.php&quot;&gt;</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-136-get-method-471"><h1 id="136-get-method-471">136. GET Method</h1><p>GET sends data as query parameters in the URL.</p><p>Characteristics:</p><ul><li>data visible in URL</li><li>URL length is limited</li><li>suitable for small amounts of data</li><li>can be cached</li><li>same URL requests generally produce the same effect</li><li>useful for retrieving/searching/filtering information</li><li>not suitable for sensitive data</li></ul><p>Example concept:</p><pre><code class="language-text">/search?q=ict</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-137-post-method-472"><h1 id="137-post-method-472">137. POST Method</h1><p>POST sends data in the body of the HTTP request.</p><p>Characteristics:</p><ul><li>data not shown in URL</li><li>supports larger data</li><li>not cached by default</li><li>cannot be bookmarked in the same way as GET</li><li>suitable for form submission and data modification</li><li>supports file uploads</li></ul><p>Security considerations:</p><ul><li>validate and sanitize input</li><li>use HTTPS</li><li>avoid exposing sensitive information through GET</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-138-get-vs-post-473"><h1 id="138-get-vs-post-473">138. GET vs POST</h1><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>GET</th><th>POST</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Main purpose</td><td>Retrieve/read data</td><td>Send/create/change data</td></tr><tr><td>Common use</td><td>Search/filter/view</td><td>Login/registration/submission/upload</td></tr><tr><td>Data location</td><td>URL query string</td><td>Request body</td></tr><tr><td>Visible in URL</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Browser history</td><td>Usually stored</td><td>Usually not stored</td></tr><tr><td>Caching</td><td>Often cached</td><td>Usually not cached</td></tr><tr><td>Sensitive data</td><td>Not suitable</td><td>Safer, but HTTPS still required</td></tr><tr><td>Data size</td><td>Limited by URL</td><td>Larger data supported</td></tr><tr><td>File upload</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Database modification</td><td>Should not modify</td><td>Intended for modification</td></tr><tr><td>Refresh risk</td><td>May repeat action</td><td>Browser may warn before resubmission</td></tr><tr><td>SEO friendly</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-139-php-superglobal-variables-474"><h1 id="139-php-superglobal-variables-474">139. PHP Superglobal Variables</h1><p>Superglobals are built-in PHP arrays that can be accessed from different scopes.</p><p>The source lists:</p><ul><li><code>$_GLOBALS</code></li><li><code>$_SERVER</code></li><li><code>$_GET</code></li><li><code>$_POST</code></li><li><code>$_FILES</code></li><li><code>$_COOKIE</code></li><li><code>$_SESSION</code></li><li><code>$_REQUEST</code></li><li><code>$_ENV</code></li></ul><p>The lesson focuses on the relevant syllabus items.</p><h2 id="globals-475"><code>$_GLOBALS</code></h2><p>Contains references to global variables.</p><h2 id="server-476"><code>$_SERVER</code></h2><p>Contains server-related information.</p><p>Examples:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$_SERVER[&#x27;PHP_SELF&#x27;]
$_SERVER[&#x27;REQUEST_METHOD&#x27;]
$_SERVER[&#x27;SERVER_NAME&#x27;]
$_SERVER[&#x27;HTTP_HOST&#x27;]
$_SERVER[&#x27;HTTP_USER_AGENT&#x27;]</code></pre><h2 id="get-477"><code>$_GET</code></h2><p>Associative array containing data passed through URL parameters.</p><p>Used with:</p><pre><code class="language-html">method=&quot;get&quot;</code></pre><h2 id="post-478"><code>$_POST</code></h2><p>Associative array containing data submitted using POST.</p><p>Used with:</p><pre><code class="language-html">method=&quot;post&quot;</code></pre><p><code>var_dump($_SERVER);</code> can be used to inspect the contents of <code>$_SERVER</code>.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-140-html-form-php-example-479"><h1 id="140-html-form-php-example-479">140. HTML Form + PHP Example</h1><p>A form can submit data to another PHP file.</p><p>Example structure:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;form method=&quot;get&quot;
      action=&quot;process.php&quot;&gt;

    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot;
           name=&quot;uname&quot;&gt;

    &lt;input type=&quot;email&quot;
           name=&quot;email&quot;&gt;

    &lt;input type=&quot;number&quot;
           name=&quot;age&quot;&gt;

    &lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot;
           name=&quot;gender&quot;
           value=&quot;Male&quot;&gt;

    &lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot;
           name=&quot;gender&quot;
           value=&quot;Female&quot;&gt;

    &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot;
           name=&quot;agree&quot;
           value=&quot;yes&quot;&gt;

    &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot;
           value=&quot;Show data&quot;&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre><p>PHP can retrieve the values:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$username = $_GET[&#x27;uname&#x27;];
$email = $_GET[&#x27;email&#x27;];
$age = $_GET[&#x27;age&#x27;];
$gender = $_GET[&#x27;gender&#x27;];</code></pre><p>Checkbox:</p><pre><code class="language-php">if (isset($_GET[&#x27;agree&#x27;])) {
    echo &quot;You have checked the agreed button&quot;;
} else {
    echo &quot;You have not checked the agreed button&quot;;
}</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-141-working-with-databases-480"><h1 id="141-working-with-databases-480">141. Working with Databases</h1><p>After processing form data, PHP can store it in a database.</p><p>PHP database interaction can use:</p><ul><li>PDO</li><li>MySQLi</li></ul><p>The source uses <strong>MySQLi</strong> for the practical examples.</p><p>PHP can communicate with database systems such as:</p><ul><li>MySQL</li><li>PostgreSQL</li><li>SQLite</li></ul><p>Database connection normally requires:</p><ul><li>server/hostname</li><li>database name</li><li>username</li><li>password</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-142-storing-form-data-481"><h1 id="142-storing-form-data-481">142. Storing Form Data</h1><p>General process:</p><ol><li>Collect form data.</li><li>Validate/sanitize the data.</li><li>Connect to the database.</li><li>Prepare an SQL query.</li><li>Execute the query.</li><li>Confirm success or handle an error.</li><li>Close the database connection.</li></ol><p>Prepared statements are mentioned as a security measure against SQL injection.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-143-database-connection-procedural-mysqli-482"><h1 id="143-database-connection-procedural-mysqli-482">143. Database Connection – Procedural MySQLi</h1><p>Basic structure:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$servername = &quot;localhost&quot;;
$username = &quot;root&quot;;
$password = &quot;&quot;;
$dbname = &quot;test_database&quot;;

$conn = mysqli_connect(
    $servername,
    $username,
    $password,
    $dbname
);

if (!$conn) {
    die(&quot;Connection failed: &quot;
        . mysqli_connect_error());
}

echo &quot;Connected successfully&quot;;

mysqli_close($conn);</code></pre><h2 id="important-components-483">Important components</h2><h3 id="servername-484"><code>$servername</code></h3><p>Database server hostname/IP.</p><p>For local development, commonly:</p><pre><code class="language-text">localhost</code></pre><h3 id="username-485"><code>$username</code></h3><p>Database username. The source uses <code>root</code> as the common WAMP local-development example.</p><h3 id="password-486"><code>$password</code></h3><p>Password for the database user.</p><h3 id="dbname-487"><code>$dbname</code></h3><p>Name of the database.</p><h3 id="mysqliconnect-488"><code>mysqli_connect()</code></h3><p>Attempts to create the database connection.</p><h3 id="conn-489"><code>$conn</code></h3><p>Stores the connection result.</p><h3 id="if-conn-490"><code>if (!$conn)</code></h3><p>Checks whether the connection failed.</p><h3 id="die-491"><code>die()</code></h3><p>Displays a message and stops the script.</p><h3 id="mysqliconnecterror-492"><code>mysqli_connect_error()</code></h3><p>Returns the connection error description.</p><h3 id="mysqliclose-493"><code>mysqli_close()</code></h3><p>Closes the database connection.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-144-database-connection-oop-mysqli-494"><h1 id="144-database-connection-oop-mysqli-494">144. Database Connection – OOP MySQLi</h1><p>Object-oriented approach:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$conn = new mysqli(
    $servername,
    $username,
    $password,
    $dbname
);

if ($conn-&gt;connect_error) {
    die(&quot;Connection failed: &quot;
        . $conn-&gt;connect_error);
}

echo &quot;Connected successfully&quot;;

$conn-&gt;close();</code></pre><p>Important elements:</p><ul><li><code>new mysqli()</code></li><li><code>$conn-&gt;connect_error</code></li><li><code>$conn-&gt;close()</code></li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-145-procedural-vs-oop-database-connection-495"><h1 id="145-procedural-vs-oop-database-connection-495">145. Procedural vs OOP Database Connection</h1><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Procedural</th><th>OOP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Create connection</td><td><code>mysqli_connect()</code></td><td><code>new mysqli()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Check connection</td><td><code>if (!$conn)</code></td><td><code>if ($conn-&gt;connect_error)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Error information</td><td><code>mysqli_connect_error()</code></td><td><code>$conn-&gt;connect_error</code></td></tr><tr><td>Close</td><td><code>mysqli_close($conn)</code></td><td><code>$conn-&gt;close()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Query execution</td><td><code>mysqli_query()</code></td><td><code>$conn-&gt;query()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-146-inserting-form-data-into-database-496"><h1 id="146-inserting-form-data-into-database-496">146. Inserting Form Data into Database</h1><p>The source example uses:</p><ul><li>HTML form</li><li>POST method</li><li><code>process.php</code></li><li>MySQL table named <code>users</code></li></ul><p>Example table structure:</p><pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE users (
    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    username VARCHAR(100),
    email VARCHAR(100),
    age INT,
    gender VARCHAR(10),
    agreement VARCHAR(10)
);</code></pre><p>Form values are collected through:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$_POST</code></pre><p>The source demonstrates:</p><pre><code class="language-php">mysqli_real_escape_string()</code></pre><p>before constructing the SQL query.</p><p>This function escapes special characters in input.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-147-mysqlirealescapestring-497"><h1 id="147-mysqlirealescapestring-497">147. <code>mysqli_real_escape_string()</code></h1><p>Purpose:</p><ul><li>prepare a string for use in an SQL query</li><li>escape special characters</li><li>help prevent input from interfering with SQL execution</li></ul><p>The source explains that user input should be sanitized/escaped so that it is treated as data rather than executable SQL content.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-148-retrieving-data-from-a-database-498"><h1 id="148-retrieving-data-from-a-database-498">148. Retrieving Data from a Database</h1><p>General process:</p><ol><li>Connect to database.</li><li>Prepare <code>SELECT</code> query.</li><li>Execute query.</li><li>Check whether results exist.</li><li>Fetch rows.</li><li>Process/display data.</li><li>Close connection.</li></ol><p>Example query:</p><pre><code class="language-sql">SELECT * FROM users</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-149-procedural-data-retrieval-499"><h1 id="149-procedural-data-retrieval-499">149. Procedural Data Retrieval</h1><p>Important functions:</p><h2 id="mysqliquery-conn-sql-500"><code>mysqli_query($conn, $sql)</code></h2><p>Executes the SQL query.</p><p>For successful:</p><ul><li>SELECT</li><li>SHOW</li><li>DESCRIBE</li><li>EXPLAIN</li></ul><p>it returns a result object.</p><p>For successful:</p><ul><li>INSERT</li><li>UPDATE</li><li>DELETE</li></ul><p>it returns <code>TRUE</code>.</p><p>If the query fails:</p><pre><code class="language-text">FALSE</code></pre><h2 id="mysqlinumrows-result-501"><code>mysqli_num_rows($result)</code></h2><p>Returns the number of rows in a result set.</p><h2 id="mysqlifetchassoc-result-502"><code>mysqli_fetch_assoc($result)</code></h2><p>Returns the next row as an associative array.</p><p>The keys correspond to column names.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-150-oop-data-retrieval-503"><h1 id="150-oop-data-retrieval-503">150. OOP Data Retrieval</h1><p>Use:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$conn-&gt;query($sql)</code></pre><p>To check rows:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$result-&gt;num_rows</code></pre><p>To retrieve a row:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$result-&gt;fetch_assoc()</code></pre><p>The returned row is an associative array.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-151-creating-a-database-table-with-php-504"><h1 id="151-creating-a-database-table-with-php-504">151. Creating a Database Table with PHP</h1><p>The source provides a <code>CREATE TABLE</code> example.</p><p>Example structure:</p><pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE employees (
    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    first_name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    last_name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    email VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    phone VARCHAR(20),
    hire_date DATE
)</code></pre><p>PHP executes the SQL through the database connection.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-152-updating-table-data-505"><h1 id="152-updating-table-data-505">152. Updating Table Data</h1><p>The source demonstrates:</p><pre><code class="language-sql">UPDATE employees
SET email=&#x27;john.newemail@example.com&#x27;
WHERE id=1</code></pre><p>The PHP program:</p><ol><li>connects to database</li><li>executes the UPDATE query</li><li>reports success/error</li><li>closes connection</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-153-alter-table-506"><h1 id="153-alter-table-506">153. ALTER TABLE</h1><p>The source demonstrates changing the table structure using:</p><pre><code class="language-sql">ALTER TABLE employees
ADD salary DECIMAL(10,2)</code></pre><p>The PHP code executes the SQL query through the database connection.</p><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-154-deleting-table-data-507"><h1 id="154-deleting-table-data-507">154. Deleting Table Data</h1><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-sql">DELETE FROM employees
WHERE id=3</code></pre><p>The PHP program:</p><ol><li>connects</li><li>executes DELETE</li><li>reports success/error</li><li>closes the connection</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-155-retrieving-data-and-prefilling-forms-508"><h1 id="155-retrieving-data-and-prefilling-forms-508">155. Retrieving Data and Prefilling Forms</h1><p>A common PHP pattern is:</p><ol><li>Retrieve data from a database.</li><li>Put the data into a form.</li><li>Allow the user to edit it.</li><li>Submit updated values back to the server.</li></ol><p>This pattern is used for systems such as:</p><ul><li>Edit Profile</li><li>Update Student</li><li>Edit Product</li><li>Settings pages</li></ul><p>Example:</p><pre><code class="language-php">$row[&#x27;name&#x27;]
$row[&#x27;email&#x27;]</code></pre><p>These values can be placed into form fields:</p><pre><code class="language-html">&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot;
       name=&quot;name&quot;
       value=&quot;&lt;?php echo $row[&#x27;name&#x27;]; ?&gt;&quot;&gt;

&lt;input type=&quot;email&quot;
       name=&quot;email&quot;
       value=&quot;&lt;?php echo $row[&#x27;email&#x27;]; ?&gt;&quot;&gt;</code></pre><p>This is called:</p><ul><li>prefilling form fields</li><li>setting form values dynamically</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-156-important-student-revision-map-509"><h1 id="156-important-student-revision-map-509">156. Important Student Revision Map</h1><h2 id="html-510">HTML</h2><p>Remember:</p><ul><li>HTML = structure/content</li><li>browser = reads and renders HTML</li><li>tags = building blocks</li><li>elements = tags + content</li><li>container tags = opening + closing tags</li><li>empty tags = no closing tag</li><li>attributes = additional information</li><li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> = paragraph</li><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>–<code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> = headings</li><li><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code> = lists</li><li><code>&lt;table&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> = tables</li><li><code>&lt;img&gt;</code> = image</li><li><code>&lt;audio&gt;</code> = audio</li><li><code>&lt;video&gt;</code> = video</li><li><code>&lt;a&gt;</code> = hyperlink</li><li><code>href</code> = destination</li><li><code>target</code> = where link opens</li><li><code>id</code> + <code>#id</code> = bookmark</li><li><code>&lt;form&gt;</code> = form</li><li><code>action</code> = destination for form data</li><li><code>method</code> = GET/POST</li><li><code>name</code> = key used when submitting form data</li></ul><h2 id="css-511">CSS</h2><p>Remember:</p><ul><li>CSS = presentation</li><li>Inline / Internal / External</li><li>selector + declaration block</li><li>element / ID / class / universal / group selectors</li><li>compound selectors</li><li>attribute selectors</li><li>fonts</li><li>text properties</li><li>link states</li><li>list properties</li><li>spacing</li><li>backgrounds</li><li>comments</li><li>box model</li><li>borders</li><li>table styling</li><li>validation</li><li>measuring units</li></ul><h2 id="php-512">PHP</h2><p>Remember:</p><ul><li>PHP = server-side scripting</li><li><code>.php</code></li><li><code>&lt;?php ... ?&gt;</code></li><li>semicolon</li><li>variables begin with <code>$</code></li><li>variables are case-sensitive</li><li><code>echo</code> / <code>print</code></li><li>arithmetic / assignment / logical / comparison operators</li><li>pre/post increment and decrement</li><li><code>var_dump()</code></li><li>string/integer/float/boolean/array</li><li>if/else/elseif</li><li>ternary</li><li>switch</li><li>for/foreach/while/do-while</li><li>nested loops</li><li>break/continue</li><li>functions</li><li>local/global scope</li><li><code>global</code></li><li><code>$GLOBALS</code></li><li>default parameters</li><li>built-in functions</li><li>forms</li><li>GET/POST</li><li>superglobals</li><li>MySQLi</li><li>procedural/OOP database connection</li><li>INSERT</li><li>SELECT</li><li>UPDATE</li><li>DELETE</li><li>CREATE TABLE</li><li>ALTER TABLE</li><li>retrieving data into forms</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-157-recommended-static-webpage-structure-for-the-vs-code-agent-513"><h1 id="157-recommended-static-webpage-structure-for-the-vs-code-agent-513">157. Recommended Static Webpage Structure for the VS Code Agent</h1><p>Create the student webpage as a <strong>single static educational learning interface</strong> containing the following navigation:</p><ol><li>Home</li><li>HTML Basics</li><li>Web and Internet</li><li>Website Types</li><li>Website Requirements</li><li>HTML Structure</li><li>HTML Tags</li><li>Text Formatting</li><li>Paragraphs and Headings</li><li>Lists</li><li>Tables</li><li>Images</li><li>Audio and Video</li><li>Hyperlinks</li><li>Bookmarks</li><li>Forms</li><li>CSS Basics</li><li>CSS Insertion Methods</li><li>CSS Syntax</li><li>CSS Selectors</li><li>CSS Text and Fonts</li><li>CSS Links and Lists</li><li>CSS Spacing and Backgrounds</li><li>CSS Box Model</li><li>CSS Tables</li><li>CSS Units</li><li>Web Authoring Tools</li><li>Web Hosting</li><li>Local Publishing</li><li>Internet Publishing</li><li>Website Performance</li><li>PHP Introduction</li><li>Static vs Dynamic Websites</li><li>How PHP Works</li><li>Local PHP Server</li><li>PHP Syntax</li><li>PHP Variables</li><li>PHP Operators</li><li>PHP Data Types</li><li>PHP Selection</li><li>PHP Loops</li><li>PHP Functions</li><li>Variable Scope</li><li>Built-in Functions</li><li>HTML Forms with PHP</li><li>GET and POST</li><li>Superglobals</li><li>PHP + MySQL</li><li>Database Connection</li><li>Insert Data</li><li>Retrieve Data</li><li>Update Data</li><li>Delete Data</li><li>Create/Alter Tables</li><li>Dynamic Form Prefilling</li><li>Final Revision</li></ol><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-158-recommended-student-friendly-presentation-514"><h1 id="158-recommended-student-friendly-presentation-514">158. Recommended Student-Friendly Presentation</h1><p>The static webpage should make the concepts easy to understand.</p><p>For each topic use this pattern:</p><h3 id="concept-515">Concept</h3><p>One short definition.</p><h3 id="why-it-is-used-516">Why it is used</h3><p>Explain the purpose in simple language.</p><h3 id="syntax-517">Syntax</h3><p>Show the syntax in a code block.</p><h3 id="example-518">Example</h3><p>Show one source-aligned example.</p><h3 id="what-happens-519">What happens?</h3><p>Explain the result step by step.</p><h3 id="remember-520">Remember</h3><p>Show 2–5 important points.</p><h3 id="compare-521">Compare</h3><p>Use a table where the source provides comparable concepts.</p><h3 id="quick-check-522">Quick Check</h3><p>Provide a small question or thought prompt based only on the lesson content.</p><p>For code:</p><ul><li>use a monospace font</li><li>provide copy buttons if desired</li><li>visually separate HTML, CSS and PHP</li><li>do not modify the source concepts</li><li>do not introduce unrelated frameworks or advanced features</li></ul><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-159-important-relationship-to-show-visually-523"><h1 id="159-important-relationship-to-show-visually-523">159. Important Relationship to Show Visually</h1><p>The webpage should clearly show the relationship:</p><pre><code class="language-text">HTML
  ↓
Structure and Content

CSS
  ↓
Appearance and Presentation

PHP
  ↓
Server-side Processing
  ↓
Dynamic Content
  ↓
Database Interaction</code></pre><p>A second useful flow:</p><pre><code class="language-text">User / Browser
      ↓
Web Request
      ↓
Web Server
      ↓
PHP Interpreter
      ↓
MySQL Database (when required)
      ↓
Generated HTML
      ↓
Web Server Response
      ↓
Browser
      ↓
Webpage Display</code></pre><hr></section><section class="lesson-chapter" id="chapter-160-final-conceptual-summary-524"><h1 id="160-final-conceptual-summary-524">160. Final Conceptual Summary</h1><p>The lesson develops a complete progression:</p><p><strong>HTML</strong> → creates the structure and content of webpages.</p><p><strong>CSS</strong> → controls how HTML content looks and is arranged.</p><p><strong>Web Hosting</strong> → provides the server resources required to make websites available online.</p><p><strong>PHP</strong> → allows server-side processing and dynamic webpage generation.</p><p><strong>HTML Forms</strong> → collect information from users.</p><p><strong>GET/POST</strong> → transfer form data to the server.</p><p><strong>PHP Superglobals</strong> → provide access to submitted/request/server data.</p><p><strong>MySQL + PHP</strong> → allow a web application to store, retrieve, update and delete data.</p><p><strong>Dynamic Web Application</strong> → combines webpages, server-side processing and database interaction to provide changing content and interactive functionality.</p><hr><h2 id="end-of-source-based-note-525">End of Source-Based Note</h2><p>Use this document as the <strong>content source for the VS Code Agent</strong>. The Agent should transform the material into a visually clear, responsive, student-friendly static HTML learning website while preserving the concepts, terminology, examples, code structures, comparisons and sequence given above.</p></section></article>
